@node main "ScarabHive"
@{jcenter}
@{b}ScarabHive@{ub}
@{jleft}

ScarabHive is a plugin-based framework for building and running LLM agents:
a core runtime, about seventy-five plugins, a web UI, a command-line client and a
REST API, all driven by YAML configuration. This guide is the manual for
both sides: for the people who install, run and use it, and for the people
who extend it with plugins, agents and panels.

Pick a topic below, or use Contents and Browse to move around. Every
plugin has a manual of its own -- its panel, its tools and hooks with their
parameters, and how to set it up:

@{code}
Plugins
  @{"  All plugins and their manuals  " link plugins/main}

Getting started
  @{"  What is ScarabHive  " link intro}
  @{"  Quickstart  " link quickstart}
  @{"  Concepts  " link concepts}

Using ScarabHive
  @{"  The web UI  " link webui}
  @{"  Chat commands  " link commands}
  @{"  Choosing an agent  " link agents}
  @{"  Sessions  " link sessions}
  @{"  Command line (agent-cli)  " link cli}

Running and configuring
  @{"  Running the server  " link running}
  @{"  Configuration files  " link config}
  @{"  Users, roles and login  " link auth}
  @{"  LLM models and profiles  " link llm}
  @{"  External MCP servers  " link mcp}
  @{"  REST API  " link api}

Developing
  @{"  Architecture of the core  " link architecture}
  @{"  Writing a plugin  " link plugins}
  @{"  Tools  " link tools}
  @{"  Hooks  " link hooks}
  @{"  Panels and the UI kit  " link panels}
  @{"  Writing an agent  " link agentdev}
  @{"  Skills  " link skills}
  @{"  Sub-agents and the SAM  " link subagents}
  @{"  Config models and schemas  " link devconfig}
  @{"  Tests and conventions  " link testing}

About this help
  @{"  Using this help  " link help}
  @{"  Writing a guide  " link authoring}
  @{"  Beyond AmigaOS  " link markdown}
@{body}
@endnode


@node intro "What is ScarabHive"
@{h1}What is ScarabHive

ScarabHive is a framework for composing and running AI agents: a Python core
that talks to LLM providers, a plugin system that gives agents tools, a
FastAPI application with a web UI, and a command-line client. Agents are
defined in YAML rather than in code, so adding a new agent is normally a
configuration change, not a code change.

It has two layers.

@{b}The core@{ub} (@{tt}src/agent_system/@{utt}) is the runtime: the agent
loop and session handling, the LLM layer, the tool server framework, plugin
discovery, the configuration models, the FastAPI application with the web
UI, lifecycle hooks, authentication and the CLI. Everything else is built on
top of it; the core itself names no LLM provider. See
@{" Architecture of the core " link architecture}.

@{b}Plugins@{ub} (@{tt}src/plugins/@{utt}, and any other folder listed in
@{tt}plugins.plugin_dirs@{utt}) supply what an agent can actually do: file
operations, a terminal, web search, memory, sequential thinking, sub-agent
orchestration, debugging tools, and the LLM providers themselves. A plugin
can provide tools, hooks, a web panel, or ship agent definitions of its own.
See @{" Writing a plugin " link plugins} and the
@{" list of installed plugins " link plugins/main}.

Agents can call other agents as tools (sub-agents), hold a persistent,
multi-turn session, and stream their progress over Server-Sent Events to the
web UI or the terminal while they work. Tools can also come from external
servers that speak the Model Context Protocol (MCP).

Beyond the chat, a fixed workflow can be a state machine whose steps are
agents, tools and Python functions (@{" stategraph " link stategraph/main}),
and every agent can be called as a model through an OpenAI-compatible API
(@{" openai_api " link openai_api/main}).
@endnode


@node quickstart "Quickstart"
@{h1}Quickstart

@{h2}1. Install

ScarabHive needs Python 3.11 or newer. No Node.js: the web UI is served by
the Python application itself. The simplest way is the install script in the
repository folder:

@{code bash}
./install.sh                                            # Linux, macOS, Git Bash
powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File install.ps1    # Windows PowerShell
@{body}

It installs everything into @{tt}.venv@{utt}, gives this installation its
own signing key (kept in @{tt}config/local.env@{utt}, named in
@{tt}config/local.yaml@{utt}), asks for a password for the @{tt}admin@{utt}
account (Enter generates one) and starts the server. Run it again after a
@{tt}git pull@{utt}: it installs what was added.
Docker and troubleshooting: @{tt}INSTALLATION.md@{utt}. By hand:

@{code bash}
python -m venv .venv
.venv\\Scripts\\activate          # Windows (cmd, PowerShell)
source .venv/bin/activate       # Linux, macOS
pip install -e .
@{body}

@{tt}pyproject.toml@{utt} also defines the optional extras @{tt}dev@{utt},
@{tt}test@{utt} and @{tt}gpu@{utt}, for example
@{tt}pip install -e ".[dev,test]"@{utt} for a contributor.

@{h2}2. API keys

Keys never go into the YAML files. Copy
@{tt}config/secrets.env.example@{utt} to @{tt}config/secrets.env@{utt} and
fill in the keys of the providers you use (@{tt}OPENROUTER_API_KEY@{utt},
@{tt}OPENAI_API_KEY@{utt}, @{tt}ANTHROPIC_API_KEY@{utt},
@{tt}GOOGLE_API_KEY@{utt}, ...). The configuration refers to them as
@{tt}${NAME}@{utt}. A variable already set in the environment wins over
@{tt}secrets.env@{utt}; one that is set nowhere becomes an empty string, and
the start logs one warning naming every missing variable. Details:
@{" Configuration files " link config}.

@{h2}3. Start the server

@{code bash}
agent-api
@{body}

This starts the FastAPI application with the web UI and the REST API. It
reads @{tt}config/config.yaml@{utt} of the project (or @{tt}AGENT_CONFIG_PATH@{utt}) and listens on
@{tt}network.host@{utt} and @{tt}network.port@{utt} from that file
(@{tt}127.0.0.1:8000@{utt} as shipped); the environment variables
@{tt}HOST@{utt} and @{tt}PORT@{utt} override them. More in
@{" Running the server " link running}.

@{h2}4. Log in

Open @{tt}http://127.0.0.1:8000@{utt}. The shipped configuration has
authentication switched on, so the browser is sent to the login page. On its
very first start, with an empty user database, the server creates one admin
account from @{tt}auth.default_admin_username@{utt},
@{tt}auth.default_admin_password@{utt} and
@{tt}auth.default_admin_email@{utt} in @{tt}config/config.yaml@{utt}. The
install scripts give it a password before that start (they ask for one, or
generate one and show it). Without them the server generates one and writes
it to the console, not into the API log. Change a generated password at once:
user menu, @{b}Settings@{ub}.

Then open the @{b}Setup@{ub} panel (admin only; panel starter or Ctrl+K). It
lists every @{tt}${NAME}@{utt} the configuration uses as @{i}set@{ui},
@{i}missing@{ui} or @{i}placeholder@{ui}, says whether the admin still has a
publicly known password and whether login tokens are signed with a known key
(@{tt}auth.secret_key@{utt} -- set your own), and it can send one test
request through the default agent.

@{h2}5. Chat

Choose an agent and a model profile with the two buttons under the message
box, type, and send with @{b}Ctrl+Enter@{ub} (Enter alone makes a new line).
See @{" The web UI " link webui} and @{" Chat commands " link commands}.

Or from a terminal, without the browser:

@{code bash}
agent-cli chat                      # interactive, with the default agent
agent-cli run "What can you do?"    # one task, then exit
agent-cli chat --agent NAME         # another agent
@{body}

@{tt}agent-cli run@{utt} and @{tt}agent-cli chat@{utt} run the agent in
their own process with the same configuration and the same session store;
@{tt}agent-api@{utt} does not have to be running. See
@{" Command line (agent-cli) " link cli}.
@endnode


@node concepts "Concepts"
@{h1}Concepts

The pieces below recur throughout this guide. Each links to the node that
goes into more detail.

@{b}Agent@{ub}: a configured assistant with a system prompt, a chain of LLM
profiles, a tool allowlist and a step budget. Most agents are pure
configuration (YAML), not code. Using one: @{" Choosing an agent " link agents};
defining one: @{" Writing an agent " link agentdev}.

@{b}Session@{ub}: the stored conversation between a user and an agent --
its messages, the agent and LLM profile in use, and metadata. Survives
restarts. See @{" Sessions " link sessions}.

@{b}Plugin@{ub}: a folder of installable functionality. Most plugins are tool
servers; others provide hooks, a web panel, agent definitions, or an LLM
provider -- or several of these at once. See @{" Writing a plugin " link plugins}.

@{b}Tool@{ub}: one function a tool server offers the model, described by a
JSON schema the model reads as its contract. See @{" Tools " link tools}.

@{b}Hook@{ub}: plugin code that runs at a fixed point of the agent loop
(before an LLM call, after one, at session start, ...) and may change what is
sent or what came back. See @{" Hooks " link hooks}.

@{b}Skill@{ub}: a packaged piece of agent knowledge -- a SKILL.md with
optional reference files -- that an agent's configuration names, instead of
copying the text into every system prompt. See @{" Skills " link skills}.

@{b}Sub-agent@{ub}: an agent that another agent starts and talks to through a
tool, with its own session and history. Orchestrated by the
@{i}sub_agent_manager@{ui} plugin (SAM). See @{" Sub-agents and the SAM " link subagents}.

@{b}LLM provider / profile@{ub}: a provider is a plugin that talks to one kind
of API (OpenAI, Anthropic, Gemini, OpenRouter, Ollama, OpenAI-compatible
servers); a profile is a short name for a configured model that agents and
users choose. See @{" LLM models and profiles " link llm}.

@{b}Panel@{ub}: a page the core or a plugin adds to the web UI, opened from
the panel catalog and docked next to the chat or floated as a window. See
@{" The web UI " link webui}.
@endnode


@node webui "The web UI"
@{h1}The web UI

The browser UI is one page around a chat. Every other view -- the system
status, a session's details, the settings, this help and every plugin's
panel -- is a page of its own, shown in a frame: docked as a tab next to the
chat, or floated as a window you can drag. A panel is open at most once;
opening it again brings it to the front.

@{h2}The screen

@{table}
Part | What is there
Header | the sessions toggle; the title of the open session (opens it in the Session panel); the search field (the command palette, Ctrl+K); on the right first the work area -- the panel starter (panels by category, with a search, pins and the recently opened) and the dock toggle (Ctrl+Alt+B) --, then the app -- for admins the buttons to restart and to stop the server, the help button (this manual) and, last, the user menu (the theme: light, dark or system; @{b}Settings@{ub}, @{b}System@{ub}, @{b}Log out@{ub})
Sessions, left | your sessions, with their sub-agent sessions: on top your folders, below the sessions in none; both grouped by day -- Today, Yesterday and This week open, each month before folded (what you open or close stays so). A folder files the sessions of the agent groups it claims by itself; a plugin brings its own (the writer: Writer, the video machines: Video), and you can change or delete those like your own; move a session by dragging it onto a folder or with its folder button, out again onto the list below. The folder button above the list makes a folder: a name, an icon, the groups it files; its pencil changes or deletes it (its sessions stay). @{b}New@{ub}, rename, delete; a marker on sessions -- and closed folders -- that are running right now. Ctrl+B hides it; its edge can be dragged
Chat, middle | the conversation; while the agent works, a progress line per tool call, which ends in the tool's result line
Message box | the text field; under it the @{b}Agent@{ub}, @{b}Model profile@{ub} and @{b}Thinking@{ub} buttons, attach, send, and @{b}Stop@{ub} while a run is going
Dock, right | docked panels as tabs; drag a tab or use Shift+Left/Right to reorder; a button opens the panel in its own browser tab. Ctrl+Alt+B hides the dock
Windows | floating panels, above everything else
Status bar | connection, the open windows, the version
@{body}

On a narrow screen the sessions list becomes an overlay and a floating panel
fills the screen.

@{h2}Chatting

@{bullet}Send with @{b}Ctrl+Enter@{ub}; Enter and Shift+Enter make a new
line.
@{bullet}A message sent while the agent is still working is added to the
running turn.
@{bullet}@{b}Stop@{ub} cancels the running turn.
@{bullet}Attach files with the paper clip, by dropping them on the message
box, or by pasting: images, audio and text, up to 20 MB each.
@{bullet}The agent and the model profile are chosen with the buttons
under the message box, see @{" Choosing an agent " link agents}. The
@{b}Thinking@{ub} button next to them sets the thinking level of this
chat, or leaves the model's own (@{tt}/think@{utt} does the same).
@{bullet}Arrow-Up in an empty line, or at the top of the text, brings back
what you sent before in this session; Arrow-Down walks forward again.
@{bullet}Typing @{tt}/@{utt} opens the list of commands; Tab or Enter
completes the one picked.
@{bullet}An agent with tool approval switched on asks before a tool runs:
the call appears under the run with its buttons, see
@{" Tool Approval " link tool_approval/main}.
@{bullet}A sub-agent's run is shown under the call that started it. What is
folded -- a run's steps, thinking, sub-agents, their answers -- is set per
account in @{b}Settings@{ub}.
@{bullet}Slash commands work in the message box, see
@{" Chat commands " link commands}.
@{bullet}An answer is drawn from the Markdown the agent wrote: headings,
lists, tables, code with its colours; a link opens in a new browser tab.
HTML in an answer shows as text, and an image in it is not loaded. An answer
that is JSON shows as it is.

@{h2}Finding a panel

All of these read the same catalog (@{tt}GET /api/ui/catalog@{utt}), and
each shows only the panels your role may open:

@{bullet}the panel starter in the header: categories and a search;
@{bullet}the command palette, @{b}Ctrl+K@{ub}: actions, panels, agents and
sessions;
@{bullet}links in the chat: a session's panel button (in the session list
and next to the attach button), its title, the request id under an answer
-- they open the panels that can show that session or that request. For
a session the menu leaves out the plugin panels that say they hold
nothing for it;
@{b}All session panels@{ub} at its end shows the others;
@{bullet}the user menu: @{b}Settings@{ub} and @{b}System@{ub};
@{bullet}a direct link: @{tt}/?panel=<panel path>@{utt}.

@{h2}Panels that come with the core

@{table}
Panel | Shows
Session | context variables, message counts and the token estimate of the active session
System | the status with its reasons, the deployed commit, running requests, tool servers and external MCP servers
Settings | theme; chat folding; your profile and password
Help | this manual and every plugin's guide
UI Kit | every standard control in every state, for panel authors
Security Audit | (admin; with authentication and @{tt}auth.endpoint_security.audit_enabled@{utt}) every request, who sent it and what it was answered
Performance, Memory Profile | (admin; only when started with @{tt}AGENT_ENABLE_PROFILING=1@{utt} or @{tt}AGENT_ENABLE_MEMORY_PROFILING=1@{utt}) profiling
@{body}

Plugins add more -- among them @{b}Setup@{ub}, @{b}Users@{ub},
@{b}Agent Editor@{ub} (admin) and @{b}Session Archive@{ub}. Every plugin with
its documentation: @{" Browse installed plugins " link plugins/main}.

@{h2}Keys

@{table}
Key | Does
Ctrl+K | command palette
Ctrl+B | show or hide the sessions list
Ctrl+Alt+B | show or hide the panel dock
Ctrl+Enter | send
Arrow-Up, Arrow-Down | at the edge of the text: earlier messages of this session
Escape | close the palette, a picker, a menu or a dialog
Shift+Left, Shift+Right | move the focused dock tab
@{body}

@{h2}What is remembered

The theme is kept in this browser. The arrangement of docked and floating
panels is kept in this browser, per account; the panel starter's pins and
recently opened panels are kept in this browser. The chat folding settings
belong to the account and follow you to other browsers.
@endnode


@node commands "Chat commands"
@{h1}Chat commands

Commands are typed into the message box, in the web UI as in
@{tt}agent-cli chat@{utt}. Most of them run without an LLM call and cost no
tokens; @{tt}/retry@{utt} and a skill send a message to the agent. Both
surfaces read the same catalogue (@{tt}src/agent_system/chat_commands.py@{utt}).

@{table}
Command | What it does
@{tt}/new@{utt} | start a fresh session; the current one stays saved
@{tt}/session@{utt} | show the current session and how to resume it
@{tt}/sessions [count\|all]@{utt} | list recent sessions of the chat agents
@{tt}/resume [id\|title]@{utt} | continue an earlier session; bare: the last one
@{tt}/title [text]@{utt} | name this session; bare: show the name
@{tt}/agent [name]@{utt} | bare: list the agents; a name switches and starts a new session
@{tt}/vars [KEY=VALUE ...]@{utt} | session variables: bare lists, @{tt}KEY=VALUE@{utt} sets, @{tt}unset KEY@{utt}, @{tt}clear@{utt}
@{tt}/tools [filter]@{utt} | the tools this agent really has, not what it claims
@{tt}/skills@{utt} | the skills you can run, and what this agent loads
@{tt}/costs@{utt} (@{tt}/cost@{utt}) | cost of the session so far, sub-agents included
@{tt}/context@{utt} (@{tt}/ctx@{utt}) | what fills this session's context window
@{tt}/history [n]@{utt} (@{tt}/hist@{utt}) | the last n exchanges (default 6)
@{tt}/last@{utt} | the tool calls and results of the last turn, in full
@{tt}/undo [files]@{utt} | drop the last exchange from the session; @{tt}files@{utt} also puts back the files it changed
@{tt}/retry [files]@{utt} | drop the last exchange and ask it again
@{tt}/rewind [n]@{utt} | put files back as they were before a checkpoint, the conversation stays; bare lists them. Checkpoints come from the @{" file_checkpoints " link file_checkpoints/main} plugin
@{tt}/export [path]@{utt} | write the conversation to a Markdown file
@{tt}/model [profile]@{utt} (@{tt}/llm@{utt}) | bare: list the LLM profiles; a name switches this session's profile. In the browser it works through the @{b}Model profile@{ub} button
@{tt}/think [level\|default]@{utt} (@{tt}/reasoning@{utt}) | the thinking level of this session: bare shows it, @{tt}default@{utt} takes the model's own. In the browser: the @{b}Thinking@{ub} button
@{tt}/copy@{utt} | copy the last answer to the clipboard
@{tt}/attach [path \| clear]@{utt} | attach a file to the next message; bare lists, @{tt}clear@{utt} empties; in the browser a path opens the file picker
@{tt}/edit [text]@{utt} | write the next message in @{tt}$EDITOR@{utt}; in the browser, in the message box
@{tt}/help [topic]@{utt} (@{tt}/h@{utt}, @{tt}/?@{utt}) | the list; with a topic, the help on it
@{body}

@{b}Terminal only@{ub} (@{tt}agent-cli chat@{utt}):

@{table}
Command | What it does
@{tt}/exit@{utt} (@{tt}/quit@{utt}, @{tt}/q@{utt}, @{tt}/bye@{utt}) | end the chat; Ctrl-D (on Windows Ctrl-Z, Enter) works too
@{body}

@{h2}Commands from plugins and skills

@{bullet}A plugin can declare commands in its @{tt}schema.yaml@{utt}; each
runs one of its tools directly, without an LLM call. Among them
@{tt}/subagents@{utt} and @{tt}/cancel@{utt} of the Sub-Agent Manager. A
command is offered only when the current agent
may call that tool. When two plugins use the same name, it is
@{tt}/<plugin>:name@{utt}. See @{" Tools " link tools}.
@{bullet}Every discovered skill runs as @{tt}/skill-name arguments@{utt}:
its text becomes the message. See @{" Skills " link skills}.
@{bullet}When names collide, a built-in command wins over a plugin command,
and a plugin command over a skill.

@{h2}Cancelling in the terminal

Ctrl-C while the agent works cancels the turn, not the chat; a second
Ctrl-C forces it. At the empty prompt, Ctrl-C twice in a row ends the chat.
@endnode


@node agents "Choosing an agent"
@{h1}Choosing an agent

An agent is a configured assistant: its own system prompt, its own tools, its
own chain of LLM profiles. Several are installed; which ones you can pick
depends on how each is configured.

@{b}Which agent runs@{ub}: the one you chose, else the one the session was
started with, else @{tt}default_agent@{utt} from
@{tt}config/config.yaml@{utt} (the shipped configuration names
@{tt}chat_agent@{utt}).

@{b}In the web UI@{ub}: the @{b}Agent@{ub} button under the message box
lists every agent whose @{tt}metadata.visibility@{utt} is @{tt}ui@{utt} or
@{tt}both@{utt} and that your role may run (an agent can require a
minimum role, @{tt}metadata.min_role@{utt}); the command palette (Ctrl+K)
lists them too. The choice
applies to the next message. @{tt}/agent NAME@{utt} switches and starts a
new session, so a conversation does not mix two agents' prompts and tools;
@{tt}/agent@{utt} alone lists the agents. Opening a session from the list
switches to the agent it belongs to. The @{b}Model profile@{ub} button next
to it picks the LLM profile, see @{" LLM models and profiles " link llm}.

@{b}In the terminal@{ub}: @{tt}agent-cli chat --agent NAME@{utt} or
@{tt}agent-cli run "task" --agent NAME@{utt}; @{tt}/agent NAME@{utt} inside
@{tt}agent-cli chat@{utt} switches and starts a new session;
@{tt}--llm PROFILE@{utt} and @{tt}/model PROFILE@{utt} choose the LLM
profile. See @{" Command line (agent-cli) " link cli}.

@{b}What an agent can do@{ub}: @{tt}/tools@{utt} shows the tools this agent
really has, not what it says it has; @{tt}/skills@{utt} shows the skills it
loads. Agents with visibility @{tt}tool@{utt} are not offered in the
picker: they are sub-agents that other agents start, see
@{" Sub-agents and the SAM " link subagents}.

Defining a new agent is a YAML file, see @{" Writing an agent " link agentdev}.
@endnode


@node sessions "Sessions"
@{h1}Sessions

A session is the stored conversation between a user and an agent: its
messages, the agent and LLM profile in use, the session's template variables
(@{tt}/vars@{utt}) and metadata such as message and token counts. Each
session is one JSON file, @{tt}sessions/<user>/<session id>.json@{utt} in
the data directory, so a conversation survives a restart and can be resumed
later by its id or its title.

@{h2}Whose sessions

Sessions belong to a user. The web UI uses the logged-in account (the name
@{tt}anonymous@{utt} when authentication is off); @{tt}agent-cli@{utt} uses
@{tt}cli_user@{utt} unless @{tt}--session-user@{utt} names another. A
session that a running process holds is locked; @{tt}--force@{utt} takes it
over from a process that got stuck.

@{h2}Running sessions and waking

With @{tt}session_presence.enabled@{utt} (on as shipped) a process that
works on a session holds an OS lock on @{tt}<session id>.lock@{utt} next to
the session file. The sessions list marks such a session as running, like
one the server itself is running, and the lock keeps two processes from
writing the same conversation: the second one is told the session is busy. The OS lets go of the lock however the
process ends, so a crashed run never looks running.

Input that arrives for a session nobody holds -- a message, a background
sub-agent that finished -- @{b}wakes@{ub} it: a new @{tt}agent-cli@{utt}
process continues the session on its stored agent and profile. A woken run
that wakes another counts one level deeper; at
@{tt}session_presence.max_wake_depth@{utt} (default 3, 0 = never wake) it
wakes nobody, so sessions that answer each other cannot start each other
forever. A session whose last run its user stopped is not woken; the input
waits for the next run somebody starts.

@{h2}Working with them

@{table}
Where | How
Web UI | the sessions list on the left: grouped by day, @{b}New@{ub}, rename (pencil), delete (bin), a marker on running sessions
Chat | @{tt}/new@{utt}, @{tt}/session@{utt}, @{tt}/sessions@{utt}, @{tt}/resume@{utt}, @{tt}/title@{utt}, @{tt}/export@{utt} -- see @{" Chat commands " link commands}
Terminal | @{tt}agent-cli chat --session ID@{utt}, @{tt}--list-sessions [N\|all]@{utt}, @{tt}--session-title TEXT@{utt} -- see @{" Command line " link cli}
API | @{tt}/api/sessions@{utt} -- see @{" REST API " link api}
@{body}

@{h2}Sub-agent sessions

A sub-agent keeps its own session, filed under the session that started it.
The sessions list shows them as sub-sessions, and the chat shows a
sub-agent's run under the call that started it.

@{h2}The archive

Old conversations move out of the live store into zip files, a whole tree at
a time (a session and all its sub-agent sessions), once @{b}every@{ub}
session in the tree is older than the retention window. It is switched on as
shipped. The keys under @{tt}session_archive:@{utt} in
@{tt}config/config.yaml@{utt}:

@{table}
Key | Shipped | Meaning
@{tt}enabled@{utt} | @{tt}true@{utt} | run the periodic sweep
@{tt}retention_days@{utt} | @{tt}30@{utt} | the age a whole tree must reach
@{tt}sweep_interval_hours@{utt} | @{tt}24.0@{utt} | how often the sweep runs
@{tt}first_sweep_delay_seconds@{utt} | @{tt}300.0@{utt} | the wait after the start
@{tt}max_trees_per_sweep@{utt} | @{tt}0@{utt} | 0 = no cap
@{tt}archive_path@{utt} | unset | default: @{tt}session_archive@{utt} in the data directory
@{body}

Changes need a restart. Restore a tree in the @{b}Session Archive@{ub}
panel, with @{tt}agent-cli run --restore-session ID@{utt}, or with
@{tt}POST /plugins/session_archive/archived/{id}/restore@{utt}
(@{" Session Archive " link session_archive/main}).
@{tt}agent-cli run --list-archived [N]@{utt} lists the archive;
@{tt}--archive-sessions [DAYS]@{utt} runs a sweep by hand, and with
@{tt}--dry-run@{utt} only reports what it would move.

@{h2}For developers: what a message carries

Besides role and content, a stored message can carry fields that are
@{b}never sent to a provider@{ub} (@{tt}PRIVATE_MESSAGE_FIELDS@{utt} in
@{tt}src/agent_system/llm/models.py@{utt}), among them
@{tt}injected_by@{utt} (set on every message the loop or a plugin inserted;
absent means a person wrote it), @{tt}request_id@{utt} (the run that
produced it) and @{tt}step@{utt}.
@endnode


@node cli "Command line (agent-cli)"
@{h1}Command line

@{table}
Program | What it is
@{tt}agent-api@{utt} | the server: web UI, REST API, all enabled plugins. See @{" Running the server " link running}
@{tt}agent-cli@{utt} | the command-line client, below
@{tt}agent-run@{utt} | a lighter one-shot runner: @{tt}agent-run "task"@{utt} with the default agent or @{tt}--agent NAME@{utt}; @{tt}agent-run --help@{utt} lists its options
@{tt}validate-agents@{utt} | checks agent YAML files, see @{" Writing a plugin " link plugins}
@{body}

@{tt}agent-cli run@{utt} and @{tt}agent-cli chat@{utt} run the agent in
their own process: same configuration, same session store, no running
server needed. They write their log to @{tt}logs/cli.log@{utt}.

@{h2}Global options (before the subcommand)

@{table}
Option | Effect
@{tt}--config PATH@{utt} | the configuration; default @{tt}AGENT_CONFIG_PATH@{utt}, else @{tt}config/config.yaml@{utt}
@{tt}-v@{utt}, @{tt}--verbose@{utt} | progress and debug messages on the console
@{tt}--color auto\|always\|never\|ansi\|html\|text@{utt} | how output is rendered: the answer's Markdown with colours (@{tt}auto@{utt} on a terminal; @{tt}always@{utt} and @{tt}ansi@{utt} force it), as HTML (@{tt}html@{utt}), or as the agent wrote it (@{tt}never@{utt}, @{tt}text@{utt}; @{tt}auto@{utt} into a pipe, under @{tt}NO_COLOR@{utt} or @{tt}TERM=dumb@{utt}); @{tt}--no-color@{utt} = @{tt}never@{utt}
@{tt}--show-tools@{utt} | show tool calls and their results
@{tt}--no-status@{utt} | hide the status lines while a run works
@{tt}--raw@{utt} | print the raw JSON result
@{body}

These may also stand after the subcommand, except after @{tt}users@{utt}. A first word that is no
subcommand is a task: @{tt}agent-cli "task"@{utt} is
@{tt}agent-cli run "task"@{utt}. @{tt}agent-cli@{utt} alone prints the
help.

@{h2}run and chat

@{code bash}
agent-cli run "task" [options]
agent-cli chat ["first message"] [options]
@{body}

Both take the same options:

@{table}
Option | Effect
@{tt}--agent NAME@{utt} | the agent; default: the session's, else @{tt}default_agent@{utt}
@{tt}--llm PROFILE@{utt} | the LLM profile
@{tt}--llm-params KEY=VALUE ...@{utt} | LLM parameters for this run, laid over the profile (@{tt}thinking_level=max max_tokens=16384@{utt}); values are typed (number, true/false, none); an unknown key is an error
@{tt}--max-steps N@{utt} | the step budget for this run (at least 1); nothing is written to the YAML
@{tt}--session ID@{utt} | continue a session
@{tt}--session-user USER@{utt} | whose sessions (default @{tt}cli_user@{utt})
@{tt}--session-title TEXT@{utt} | name the session
@{tt}--force@{utt} | take over a session a stuck process still holds
@{tt}--vars KEY=VALUE ...@{utt} | session template variables (also @{tt}--template-vars@{utt})
@{tt}--attach PATH ...@{utt} | attach files; image, audio or text, detected per file
@{tt}--list-sessions [N\|all]@{utt} | list this user's sessions of the chat agents (0 = no limit; @{tt}all@{utt} adds the runs pipelines started); sub-agent sessions are not listed
@{tt}--list-archived [N]@{utt} | list archived sessions
@{tt}--restore-session ID@{utt} | bring an archived tree back
@{tt}--archive-sessions [DAYS]@{utt} | archive this user's trees older than DAYS now (default: @{tt}session_archive.retention_days@{utt})
@{tt}--dry-run@{utt} | with @{tt}--archive-sessions@{utt}: report, change nothing
@{body}

Without a task, @{tt}run@{utt} asks "What can you do?".

Ctrl-C cancels a running turn; see @{" Chat commands " link commands} for
the commands inside @{tt}agent-cli chat@{utt}. The exit code is 1 when the
run could not start (unknown agent or profile, a session it may not use, a
rejected attachment) and when it ends with errors -- a script or a batch
that checks the code sees a failed run as failed. A cancelled run exits with 0.

@{h2}The other subcommands

@{code bash}
agent-cli plugins [list|info|search] [NAME] [--format json|table] [--show-metadata]
agent-cli reload [--url URL] [--api-key KEY] [--timeout SEC] [--format json|table]
agent-cli mcp list | status [SERVER] | test SERVER | tools SERVER
agent-cli hooks [list|inspect] [NAME] [--type TYPE] [--format json|table]
agent-cli users list | create | delete | update | info | generate-api-key | revoke-api-key
@{body}

@{bullet}@{tt}plugins@{utt}: what was discovered. @{tt}info NAME@{utt}
takes a plugin type (folder name) and shows its manifest and whether any
instance is enabled -- not the effective configuration of an entry.
@{bullet}@{tt}reload@{utt}: asks a running server to read its
configuration again, see @{" Running the server " link running}.
@{bullet}@{tt}mcp@{utt}: looks at the external MCP servers with
connections of its own, see @{" External MCP servers " link mcp}.
@{bullet}@{tt}hooks@{utt}: the registered hooks, see
@{" Hooks " link hooks}.
@{bullet}@{tt}users@{utt}: works directly on the user database, no running
server needed. @{tt}users create USERNAME EMAIL@{utt} takes
@{tt}--password@{utt}, @{tt}--name@{utt}, @{tt}--role user|admin|guest@{utt},
@{tt}--admin@{utt}, @{tt}--inactive@{utt}; @{tt}users update USERNAME@{utt}
takes @{tt}--email@{utt}, @{tt}--name@{utt}, @{tt}--password@{utt},
@{tt}--role@{utt}, @{tt}--activate@{utt}, @{tt}--deactivate@{utt};
@{tt}users list@{utt} (also @{tt}users@{utt} alone) takes @{tt}--limit@{utt}
(100) and @{tt}--skip@{utt}; @{tt}delete@{utt}, @{tt}info@{utt},
@{tt}generate-api-key@{utt} and @{tt}revoke-api-key@{utt} take a
USERNAME, @{tt}delete@{utt} and @{tt}revoke-api-key@{utt} also
@{tt}--force@{utt} (no question first). @{tt}create@{utt} without
@{tt}--password@{utt} asks for one. See
@{" Users, roles and login " link auth}.
@{bullet}@{tt}reload --timeout@{utt}: seconds to wait for the server
(default @{tt}network.cli_request_timeout@{utt}).

Turning a plugin or an MCP server on is an edit of the configuration, not a
command.
@endnode


@node running "Running the server"
@{h1}Running the server

@{h2}Starting

@{tt}agent-api@{utt} starts the FastAPI application: the web UI, the REST
API and every enabled plugin. It reads @{tt}config/config.yaml@{utt}
of the project, or the file @{tt}AGENT_CONFIG_PATH@{utt} names (as
@{tt}agent-cli@{utt} does), and takes no command-line options. Environment
variables it reads:

@{table}
Variable | Effect
@{tt}HOST@{utt}, @{tt}PORT@{utt} | override @{tt}network.host@{utt} and @{tt}network.port@{utt} (shipped: @{tt}127.0.0.1@{utt}, @{tt}8000@{utt})
@{tt}AGENT_LOG_LEVEL@{utt} | override the log level of the server process
@{tt}AGENT_DATA_DIR@{utt} | move the data directory, see @{" Configuration files " link config}
@{tt}AGENT_ENABLE_PROFILING@{utt}, @{tt}AGENT_ENABLE_MEMORY_PROFILING@{utt} | set to @{tt}1@{utt}: the admin-only Performance and Memory Profile panels and their @{tt}/debug/...@{utt} endpoints
@{body}

@{tt}agent-cli@{utt} and @{tt}agent-run@{utt} change into the project
folder when they start, so relative paths mean the project, wherever the
command was typed. @{tt}127.0.0.1@{utt} accepts connections from this machine only; set
@{tt}network.host@{utt} (or @{tt}HOST@{utt}) to serve others.
@{tt}network.remote_paths@{utt} (a list of exact paths, unset as shipped)
then narrows what a client on another machine may reach -- a webhook, say:
every other path answers it 404, before any other check. Clients on this
machine (loopback) are not affected; unset, nothing is restricted.

@{h2}Logs

@{tt}logging:@{utt} in @{tt}config.yaml@{utt}: the server writes
@{tt}logging.file_api@{utt} (@{tt}logs/api.log@{utt} as shipped),
@{tt}agent-cli@{utt} writes @{tt}logging.file_cli@{utt}
(@{tt}logs/cli.log@{utt}), so the two never share a file. The files rotate
by @{tt}max_bytes@{utt} and @{tt}backup_count@{utt}. Missing
@{tt}${NAME}@{utt} variables and hooks that failed show up there. The first
admin's generated password does not: it is printed on the console only,
once. With authentication on, @{tt}logs/security.log@{utt} holds the route
audit (who called what, allowed or denied).

@{h2}Reload or restart

@{tt}agent-cli reload@{utt} (or @{tt}POST /admin/reload-config@{utt}, admin)
makes a running server read its configuration again, without dropping
sessions or runs. It changes what a plugin can take over live:

@{bullet}an agent's whole @{tt}agent_config@{utt} -- model chain, LLM
params, tools, hooks, timeouts, prompt, escalation -- and its
@{tt}description@{utt}, @{tt}metadata@{utt} (role gate, visibility) and
@{tt}self_tool_descriptions@{utt}, also for the agent a machine's
@{tt}agent:@{utt} block declares;
@{bullet}the LLM models and profiles of @{tt}llm.yaml@{utt}: an agent builds
its new client in the reload itself, the LLM-using hooks (summarizer,
watchdog, continuation, tool-result summaries) on their first use after the
change, a machine step with its own @{tt}llm_profile@{utt} with the next
run; an unchanged profile keeps its client. A run in flight keeps the model
it started on; the rest it reads from its next step on;
@{bullet}a SAM's @{tt}allowed_agents@{utt}, @{tt}blocked_agents@{utt},
@{tt}allow_advanced_model@{utt}, @{tt}advanced_create_only_agents@{utt},
@{tt}advanced_continue_only_agents@{utt} and its limits;
@{bullet}a stategraph instance's semaphores.

A profile that does not build fails the reload for the agents on it; they
keep running as they were, and the report lists them under errors.
Everything else needs a @{b}restart@{ub} of @{tt}agent-api@{utt}: a new or
removed server entry, a changed @{tt}type@{utt}, the settings of plugins
without a reload (the report lists them as unsupported) -- also the models
of the tool servers that keep their own client from the start
(@{tt}decision@{utt}, @{tt}llm_router@{utt}, the consolidation of
@{tt}lessons_learned@{utt}) --, the authentication
settings, MCP servers, @{tt}network@{utt}, batch mode,
@{tt}session_archive@{utt}, the data directory. Prompt templates need
neither: they are read from disk on every render. There is no file watcher;
a reload happens only when someone asks for it.

@{tt}agent-cli reload@{utt} talks to @{tt}http://127.0.0.1:8000@{utt}
unless @{tt}--url@{utt} or @{tt}AGENT_SERVER_URL@{utt} says otherwise, and
authenticates with @{tt}--api-key@{utt} or @{tt}AGENT_ADMIN_API_KEY@{utt} /
@{tt}AGENT_API_KEY@{utt} (the API key of an admin).

@{h2}Restart and stop from the browser

An admin has two buttons in the header, and the same two actions under
Ctrl+K:

@{bullet}@{b}Restart the server@{ub} (@{tt}POST /admin/server/restart@{utt})
stops the server and starts it again: new and changed plugins, agents,
skills and code are loaded as on any start. The page waits and loads
itself anew when the server answers again.
@{bullet}@{b}Stop the server@{ub} (@{tt}POST /admin/server/stop@{utt}) ends
it, as Ctrl+C in its terminal does. It stays down until it is started
again on its machine.

Both ask first and say how many requests are running: those end with the
server, sessions stay. The server gets 10 seconds to finish by itself and
is then hurried; a connection that stays open (a run's stream) makes a
stop take up to 20 seconds.

@{bullet}A restart needs a server started with @{tt}agent-api@{utt}: on
Windows its launcher starts it again, elsewhere the process replaces
itself. A server started another way ("uvicorn
agent_system.app:build_app") answers 409 and is restarted by whoever
started it.
@{bullet}Under a supervisor that restarts whatever ends -- Docker's
@{tt}restart: unless-stopped@{utt} of the shipped docker-compose.yml, a
service manager -- a stop comes back as a restart. Stop the container or
the service there.
@{bullet}Without authentication there are no admin routes, and so no
buttons.

@{h2}Health

@{tt}GET /health@{utt} and @{tt}GET /api/health@{utt} answer without a
login. The @{b}System@{ub} panel shows the status with its reasons, running
requests, tool servers and external MCP servers.
@endnode


@node config "Configuration files"
@{h1}Configuration files

Configuration is YAML in @{tt}config/@{utt}, assembled from
@{tt}config/config.yaml@{utt} and the files its @{tt}includes:@{utt} list
names:

@{table}
File | Holds
@{tt}config.yaml@{utt} | @{tt}includes@{utt}, @{tt}context@{utt} (date, time zone, location for prompts), @{tt}status@{utt}, @{tt}vision@{utt}, @{tt}session_presence@{utt}, @{tt}session_archive@{utt}, @{tt}network@{utt}, @{tt}default_agent@{utt}, @{tt}skills@{utt}, @{tt}auth@{utt}, @{tt}logging@{utt}; optionally @{tt}paths@{utt}
@{tt}llm.yaml@{utt} | @{tt}llm_system:@{utt} models and profiles, see @{" LLM models and profiles " link llm}
@{tt}llm_openrouter.yaml@{utt} | more @{tt}llm_system:@{utt} model entries (OpenRouter)
@{tt}plugins.yaml@{utt} | @{tt}plugins.plugin_dirs@{utt}, @{tt}plugins.default_config@{utt} (what every server entry inherits), @{tt}plugins.servers@{utt} (one entry per plugin instance), and the global @{tt}hooks:@{utt} section
@{tt}mcp_servers.yaml@{utt} | @{tt}external_servers:@{utt}, see @{" External MCP servers " link mcp}
@{tt}agents*/*.yaml@{utt} | agents and other server entries of this installation
@{tt}../src/plugins*/*/agents/*.yaml@{utt} | the agents and server entries that plugins ship
@{tt}security.yaml@{utt} | the route rules: @{tt}auth.endpoint_security@{utt}, @{tt}llm_security@{utt}, @{tt}plugin_security@{utt}
@{tt}local.yaml@{utt} | this machine's own settings, never in the repository: read after every other file, may set every section but @{tt}includes@{utt} (the signing key, the network), and if it is there it must load, or the start fails; keys of this machine are in @{tt}local.env@{utt}
@{body}

Not included, read on their own: @{tt}llm_pricing.yaml@{utt} (prices for
cost reporting), @{tt}secrets.env@{utt} (keys), and the prompt templates in
@{tt}config/prompts/@{utt}.

@{h2}How the files are merged

In an included file, @{tt}llm_system@{utt},
@{tt}plugins@{utt} and @{tt}hooks@{utt} are merged key by key with what came
before; @{tt}external_servers@{utt} is @{b}replaced@{ub} as a whole by a
later file. Every other section an include sets over what came before,
except @{tt}includes@{utt}, which only @{tt}config.yaml@{utt} sets, and
@{tt}paths@{utt} and @{tt}auth@{utt}, which @{tt}config.yaml@{utt}
and @{tt}local.yaml@{utt} set alone -- and auth's route rules, which a file @{tt}config.yaml@{utt}
names by its own path may set (@{tt}security.yaml@{utt}). A file named by its
path must exist and load, or the start fails; a file a glob matched that does
not parse is skipped with an error in the log, and the rest loads.

@{h2}Secrets: ${NAME}

Any value may contain @{tt}${NAME}@{utt} (capital letters, digits,
underscore). At load it is replaced from the environment;
@{tt}config/secrets.env@{utt} (copied from
@{tt}config/secrets.env.example@{utt}) fills in the variables the
environment does not already have -- a real environment variable always
wins. An unset variable becomes an empty string, and the start logs one
warning naming all of them. There is no default syntax
(@{tt}${NAME:-x}@{utt}).

Set your own @{tt}auth.secret_key@{utt}: it signs the login tokens. The
@{b}Setup@{ub} panel reports a key that is publicly known.

@{h2}The data directory

Everything ScarabHive writes -- sessions, the user database, plugin
databases, caches, the archive -- lies under one data directory,
@{tt}data/@{utt} in the project by default. Move it with the environment
variable @{tt}AGENT_DATA_DIR@{utt} or with @{tt}paths.data_dir@{utt} in
@{tt}config.yaml@{utt} (the environment wins; a relative path is relative to
the project). Either needs a restart. Log files are separate, see
@{" Running the server " link running}.

@{h2}Who changes what

@{tt}config/llm*.yaml@{utt} is the operator's: nothing in ScarabHive
rewrites it. The @{b}Agent Editor@{ub} panel (admin) edits agent YAML files
and keeps their comments. After an edit:
@{" reload or restart " link running}.
@endnode


@node auth "Users, roles and login"
@{h1}Users, roles and login

@{tt}auth.enabled@{utt} is switched on in the shipped
@{tt}config/config.yaml@{utt}; without the key it is off. With it off there
is a single user: every caller counts as admin for panels and routes, and
sessions are stored under the name @{tt}anonymous@{utt} -- fine for one
person on one machine, not for anything others can reach.

@{h2}Roles

@{tt}admin@{utt}, @{tt}user@{utt} and @{tt}guest@{utt}, in that order of
rank. A new account is a @{tt}user@{utt}. With
@{tt}auth.anonymous_access.enabled@{utt} (off as shipped) a caller without a
login gets @{tt}auth.anonymous_access.role@{utt} (@{tt}guest@{utt}) on the
routes in @{tt}auth.anonymous_access.allowed_endpoints@{utt}; as shipped,
@{tt}auth.llm_security@{utt} still keeps such callers from starting LLM
runs, and users can open only their own sessions.

@{h2}Accounts

@{bullet}The first admin is created on the very first start, see
@{" Quickstart " link quickstart}.
@{bullet}Admins manage accounts in the @{b}Users@{ub} panel, with
@{tt}agent-cli users@{utt} (see @{" Command line " link cli}), or through
@{tt}/admin/users@{utt}.
@{bullet}Everybody changes their own profile and password in
@{b}Settings@{ub}.
@{bullet}@{b}Forgot password?@{ub} on the login page mails a link to the
account's address, good once and for @{tt}auth.password_reset.link_minutes@{utt};
setting the password ends every login of the account. It shows once
@{tt}auth.mail@{utt} names a mail server and a sender and
@{tt}auth.password_reset.public_url@{utt} the address users reach the server
at -- the link is built on it, never on what a request claims.
@{bullet}@{b}Registration is open@{ub}: @{tt}POST /auth/register@{utt}
creates a @{tt}user@{utt} for anyone who can reach the server; as shipped
(@{tt}auth.registration.require_approval@{utt}) it stays inactive until an
admin activates it. The
login page offers no sign-up, but the route is there. To close it, set
@{tt}auth.registration.enabled: false@{utt}.

@{h2}Logging in

The browser logs in on @{tt}/login@{utt}; the token is kept in an HttpOnly
cookie (@{tt}access_token@{utt}); @{b}Log out@{ub} is in the user menu.
Programs authenticate with either

@{bullet}a bearer token from @{tt}POST /auth/login@{utt} (lifetime
@{tt}auth.access_token_expire_minutes@{utt}; the shipped configuration sets
it to years -- shorten it for anything but a local setup; renew with
@{tt}POST /auth/refresh@{utt}), or
@{bullet}a personal API key in the @{tt}X-API-Key@{utt} header, created with
@{tt}POST /auth/api-key@{utt} or @{tt}agent-cli users generate-api-key@{utt}
and revoked with @{tt}DELETE /auth/api-key@{utt} or
@{tt}revoke-api-key@{utt}.

@{h2}Which routes a role may use

Two layers decide, and both must let a caller through:

@{table}
Layer | How it decides
@{tt}auth.endpoint_security@{utt} | @{tt}rules@{utt}, read in order, the first match wins; each has a @{tt}pattern@{utt} ("METHOD /path", wildcards allowed), a @{tt}policy@{utt} (@{tt}require_auth@{utt} or @{tt}allow_anonymous@{utt}) and, for @{tt}require_auth@{utt}, a @{tt}min_role@{utt}. No match: @{tt}default_policy@{utt}
@{tt}auth.plugin_security@{utt} | for @{tt}/plugins/...@{utt}: @{tt}endpoint_rules@{utt} first, then @{tt}plugin_overrides@{utt} per plugin, then @{tt}default_policy@{utt} and @{tt}default_min_role@{utt}
@{body}

The shipped rules keep @{tt}/docs@{utt}, @{tt}/redoc@{utt},
@{tt}/openapi.json@{utt}, @{tt}/admin/*@{utt}, @{tt}/debug/*@{utt},
@{tt}/hooks@{utt}, @{tt}/tools/*@{utt} and the agent debug routes to admins,
and the Setup, Users, Agent Editor, State Graph, Forge, Logs and SSH
Machines panels too. The same two layers decide which panels the catalog offers: a
panel appears only for the roles that may open it, without anything extra in
the plugin.

@{h2}Which agents a role may run

An agent can require a minimum role with @{tt}metadata.min_role@{utt}
(@{tt}guest@{utt}, @{tt}user@{utt} or @{tt}admin@{utt}; unset: anybody).
For a caller below it the agent is missing from the picker, and a run of it
-- from the chat, @{tt}POST /run@{utt}, as a sub-agent or as another
agent's tool -- is refused as if it did not exist. The gate applies only
with authentication on. @{tt}agent-cli@{utt} and @{tt}agent-run@{utt} run
as the local operator: their default user @{tt}cli_user@{utt} passes every
gate while no account has that name.

@{h2}Other auth settings

@{table}
Key | Shipped | Meaning
@{tt}secret_key@{utt} | a public development key | signs the login tokens; set your own (the install scripts do). A published key logs an error at the start; an empty key or one under 32 characters stops it
@{tt}reject_default_secret_key@{utt} | @{tt}false@{utt} | true: refuse to start with a published key
@{tt}refresh_token_expire_days@{utt} | @{tt}3650@{utt} | lifetime of a refresh token
@{tt}database_path@{utt} | @{tt}data/users.db@{utt} | the user database
@{tt}registration.default_role@{utt} | @{tt}user@{utt} | the role of a registered account (@{tt}guest@{utt} or @{tt}user@{utt})
@{tt}mail.host@{utt}, @{tt}mail.port@{utt}, @{tt}mail.security@{utt} | unset, by security, @{tt}starttls@{utt} | the mail server the reset link goes out through. Unset, the port follows @{tt}security@{utt}: 587 for @{tt}starttls@{utt}, 465 for @{tt}ssl@{utt}, 25 for @{tt}none@{utt}. Set it where a provider blocks one, and keep @{tt}security@{utt} to the port: @{tt}ssl@{utt} speaks TLS from the first byte (465), @{tt}starttls@{utt} starts plain and switches (587, often 2525)
@{tt}mail.timeout@{utt} | @{tt}20@{utt} | seconds a step of the conversation with the mail server may take
@{tt}mail.username@{utt}, @{tt}mail.password@{utt}, @{tt}mail.sender@{utt} | unset | its login (the password as @{tt}${SMTP_PASSWORD}@{utt} from @{tt}secrets.env@{utt}) and the From address
@{tt}password_reset.public_url@{utt}, @{tt}password_reset.link_minutes@{utt} | unset, @{tt}30@{utt} | where users reach the server, and how long a reset link holds
@{tt}rate_limit_enabled@{utt}, @{tt}requests_per_minute@{utt} | @{tt}false@{utt}, @{tt}60@{utt} | requests per client and minute
@{tt}security_headers_enabled@{utt} | @{tt}true@{utt} | security headers on every answer
@{tt}cors_enabled@{utt}, @{tt}cors_origins@{utt}, @{tt}cors_credentials@{utt}, @{tt}cors_methods@{utt}, @{tt}cors_headers@{utt} | on, @{tt}*@{utt}, off | CORS for a front end on another origin; the web UI needs none
@{tt}trusted_hosts@{utt} | unset | the host names the server answers to (patterns such as @{tt}*.example.com@{utt}); unset, any
@{body}

All of them are read at the start and only from @{tt}config.yaml@{utt} or
@{tt}local.yaml@{utt}; changing one needs a restart.
@endnode


@node llm "LLM models and profiles"
@{h1}LLM models and profiles

Three layers: a @{b}provider@{ub} plugin knows how to talk to one kind of
API; a @{b}model@{ub} entry configures one model on one provider; a
@{b}profile@{ub} is a short name for a model entry. Agents and users choose
profiles, never models.

@{h2}Providers

Providers are plugins in @{tt}src/plugins/llm_*@{utt}:

@{table}
Plugin | Provider names (@{tt}provider:@{utt} in a model entry)
@{tt}llm_openai@{utt} | @{tt}openai@{utt}
@{tt}llm_anthropic@{utt} | @{tt}anthropic@{utt}
@{tt}llm_gemini@{utt} | @{tt}gemini@{utt}, @{tt}gemini_sdk@{utt}
@{tt}llm_openrouter@{utt} | @{tt}openrouter_sdk@{utt}
@{tt}llm_ollama@{utt} | @{tt}ollama@{utt}
@{tt}llm_openai_compat@{utt} | @{tt}openai_httpx@{utt}, @{tt}openai_responses@{utt} (OpenAI-compatible servers)
@{tt}llm_decisions@{utt} | small models for yes/no decisions
@{body}

@{tt}llm_common@{utt} holds the code the providers share. Some providers
also offer batch processing or text-to-speech.

@{h2}config/llm.yaml

The file is yours: the framework never changes it. Everything sits under
@{tt}llm_system:@{utt}; @{tt}config/llm_openrouter.yaml@{utt} adds more model
entries the same way.

@{table}
Section | Holds
@{tt}models@{utt} | named model entries: @{tt}provider@{utt}, @{tt}model@{utt}, @{tt}base_url@{utt}, @{tt}api_key: ${...}@{utt}, @{tt}context_window@{utt}, @{tt}max_tokens@{utt}, @{tt}capabilities@{utt}, ...; @{tt}extends: <other entry>@{utt} inherits and overrides only the differences
@{tt}profiles@{utt} | short name -> @{tt}model_ref@{utt}, @{tt}description@{utt}, optionally @{tt}max_steps@{utt}
@{tt}default_profile@{utt} | the profile used when nothing else is chosen
@{tt}batch@{utt} | batch settings per provider
@{tt}httpx_timeouts@{utt} | HTTP timeouts of the clients
@{tt}openrouter_routing@{utt} | the default OpenRouter provider routing for every model with an openrouter.ai @{tt}base_url@{utt}; a model entry's own @{tt}provider_routing@{utt} wins per key
@{tt}tts_models@{utt}, @{tt}tts_profiles@{utt} | text-to-speech
@{tt}decision_models@{utt}, @{tt}decision_profiles@{utt} | small models for yes/no decisions
@{body}

@{code yaml}
llm_system:
  models:
    my-model:
      provider: openai
      model: some-model-id
      api_key: ${OPENAI_API_KEY}
      context_window: 128000
  profiles:
    normal:
      model_ref: my-model
      description: "Everyday work"
@{body}

A model entry can also carry prompt-cache options
(@{tt}prompt_cache_key@{utt}, @{tt}prompt_cache_mode@{utt},
@{tt}prompt_cache_marker_style@{utt}, @{tt}prompt_cache_options@{utt}).

@{b}Prices@{ub} live in @{tt}config/llm_pricing.yaml@{utt} (per million
tokens), read on its own for cost reporting. Where the provider reports the
cost itself (OpenRouter), that figure wins. A model without a price entry is
reported as unknown, not as free.

@{h2}Choosing a profile

@{bullet}An agent's @{tt}agent_config.llm_profile@{utt} is a chain: the
primary profile, then fallbacks; @{tt}llm_profile_advanced@{utt} is a second
chain for harder work; @{tt}llm_params@{utt} changes parameters of the
models it uses without a model entry of its own. See
@{" Writing an agent " link agentdev}.
@{bullet}In the web UI: the @{b}Model profile@{ub} button under the message
box (the list comes from @{tt}GET /llm/profiles@{utt}).
@{bullet}In the terminal: @{tt}--llm PROFILE@{utt} on @{tt}agent-cli run@{utt}
and @{tt}chat@{utt}, @{tt}/model PROFILE@{utt} inside @{tt}agent-cli chat@{utt};
@{tt}--llm-params@{utt} for single parameters.

An agent builds its LLM client when it starts: changes to
@{tt}llm.yaml@{utt} or to an agent's profiles reach running agents only
after a restart.

@{h2}Writing a provider

@{code toml}
[plugin]
type = ["llm-provider"]            # required: the LLM registry skips anything else
provides = ["myprovider"]          # optional: provides_batch, provides_tts, provides_decisions
default_base_url = { myprovider = "https://..." }
dependencies = ["my-sdk>=1.0"]
@{body}

@{bullet}The module is always @{tt}<folder>/provider.py@{utt} with
@{tt}PROVIDERS = {name: build_fn(cfg, ssl_verify)}@{utt} (optionally
@{tt}BATCH_BACKENDS@{utt}, @{tt}TTS_PROVIDERS@{utt}), and there is no
@{tt}plugin.py@{utt}. A name declared in @{tt}provides@{utt} but not
exported fails with @{tt}ProviderNotFoundError@{utt}.
@{bullet}Clients are built only through @{tt}create_llm_from_profile@{utt};
the core names no provider and imports no provider SDK (guarded by
@{tt}tests/llm/test_llm_provider_registry.py@{utt}).
@{bullet}No provider tables (@{tt}if provider == "google"@{utt}, alias
dicts): mappings come from configuration.
@{bullet}Report usage in OpenAI terms: @{tt}prompt_tokens@{utt}
@{b}including@{ub} cached tokens, the cache details as a subset.
@endnode


@node mcp "External MCP servers"
@{h1}External MCP servers

Besides its own plugins, ScarabHive can use the tools of external servers
that speak the Model Context Protocol. The @{tt}mcp_client@{utt} plugin
(enabled as shipped) connects to them; they are configured in
@{tt}config/mcp_servers.yaml@{utt} under @{tt}external_servers@{utt}:

@{code yaml}
external_servers:
  connection: {timeout: 5.0, parallel_connect: true}
  cache: {enabled: true, tool_list_ttl: 3600.0}
  remote_servers:
    my_server:
      enabled: true
      transport: streaming          # or sse, or stdio
      url: "https://..."            # streaming and sse
      # command, args, env instead, for a local stdio server
      tools: {allowed: [], blocked: []}
      auth: {type: none}            # none | bearer | api_key | basic
@{body}

@{bullet}@{tt}transport: stdio@{utt} starts the server as a local process
(@{tt}command@{utt}, optionally @{tt}args@{utt} and @{tt}env@{utt});
@{tt}streaming@{utt} (streamable HTTP) and @{tt}sse@{utt} connect to a
@{tt}url@{utt}.
@{bullet}@{tt}enabled@{utt} defaults to false. @{tt}timeout@{utt} per
server (seconds) overrides @{tt}connection.timeout@{utt} for a slow one.
@{tt}connect: on_demand@{utt} (instead of @{tt}startup@{utt}, the
default) connects -- and for stdio starts -- the server only in a process
that runs an agent whose allowlist names it.
@{bullet}A key or token belongs in @{tt}config/secrets.env@{utt},
referenced as @{tt}${NAME}@{utt}.
@{bullet}@{b}Keep the whole section in this one file.@{ub} A later included
file that also has an @{tt}external_servers:@{utt} section replaces this one
completely instead of merging with it.

@{h2}Reaching an agent

In an agent's allowlist an external tool is written @{tt}server.tool@{utt}
-- a dot, where plugin tools have a slash:

@{code yaml}
agent_config:
  tools:
    allowed: ["my_server.*"]
@{body}

The server entry's own @{tt}tools.allowed@{utt} and @{tt}tools.blocked@{utt}
filter what it offers at all. The model sees the tool as
@{tt}my_server_<tool>@{utt}.

@{h2}Inspecting

@{code bash}
agent-cli mcp list
agent-cli mcp status [SERVER]
agent-cli mcp test SERVER
agent-cli mcp tools SERVER
@{body}

These open connections of their own, which end with the command. The
@{b}System@{ub} panel shows the external servers of the running server.
Switching a server on or off is an edit of the file and a restart. More in
the @{" mcp_client " link mcp_client/main} guide.
@endnode


@node api "REST API"
@{h1}REST API

@{tt}agent-api@{utt} serves the REST API next to the web UI. Most routes need
a login (see @{" Users, roles and login " link auth}): the
@{tt}access_token@{utt} cookie of the browser, @{tt}Authorization: Bearer
<token>@{utt}, or @{tt}X-API-Key: <key>@{utt}. The interactive documentation
is at @{tt}/docs@{utt} and @{tt}/redoc@{utt} -- admin only under the shipped
rules. The routes under @{tt}/auth@{utt}, and those under
@{tt}/admin@{utt} but @{tt}GET /admin/config@{utt}, exist only with
authentication on; with it off, @{tt}/api/sessions@{utt} acts as the user
@{tt}anonymous@{utt}. The main groups:

@{table}
Route | Purpose
@{tt}POST /events@{utt} | start a run and stream its events (SSE); what the web chat uses
@{tt}GET /events@{utt} | follow a run again, e.g. after a reload
@{tt}POST /events/{request_id}/append@{utt} | add a message to a running turn
@{tt}POST /run@{utt} | run a task; also accepts files (multipart), which the web chat uses for attachments
@{tt}GET /api/requests/{id}/status@{utt}, @{tt}POST /api/requests/{id}/cancel@{utt} | a run's state; cancel it
@{tt}POST /sessions@{utt}, @{tt}POST /sessions/{id}/append@{utt} | a new session id; add a user message to a session without a run
@{tt}/api/sessions@{utt} | @{tt}POST@{utt} create, @{tt}GET@{utt} list; also @{tt}/hierarchy@{utt}, @{tt}/active@{utt}, @{tt}/resolve@{utt}, @{tt}/listing@{utt}
@{tt}/api/sessions/{id}@{utt} | @{tt}GET@{utt}, @{tt}PUT@{utt} or @{tt}PATCH@{utt}, @{tt}DELETE@{utt}; @{tt}/messages@{utt}, @{tt}/children@{utt}, @{tt}POST /restore@{utt}
@{tt}GET /agents@{utt} | the agents for the picker
@{tt}GET /agents/{name}/tools@{utt}, @{tt}/allowed-tools@{utt} | an agent's tools
@{tt}GET /agents/debug/{name}/system-prompt@{utt}, @{tt}/allowed-tools@{utt} | what the agent finally sees (admin)
@{tt}GET /llm/profiles@{utt} | the LLM profiles
@{tt}/chat/...@{utt} | the chat commands: @{tt}GET /chat/commands@{utt}, @{tt}POST /chat/resolve@{utt}, @{tt}POST /chat/command@{utt}, @{tt}GET@{utt}/@{tt}POST /chat/vars@{utt}, @{tt}POST /chat/undo@{utt}, @{tt}GET /chat/checkpoints@{utt}, @{tt}POST /chat/rewind@{utt}, @{tt}GET /chat/context@{utt}, @{tt}GET /chat/transcript@{utt}, @{tt}GET /chat/last_answer@{utt}
@{tt}/auth/...@{utt} | @{tt}POST register@{utt}, @{tt}login@{utt}, @{tt}logout@{utt}, @{tt}refresh@{utt}; @{tt}GET@{utt}/@{tt}PATCH /auth/me@{utt}; @{tt}GET@{utt}/@{tt}PUT /auth/me/preferences@{utt}; @{tt}POST@{utt}/@{tt}DELETE /auth/api-key@{utt}
@{tt}/admin/...@{utt} | admin: @{tt}POST reload-config@{utt}, @{tt}GET config@{utt}, @{tt}users@{utt} (@{tt}GET@{utt}, @{tt}POST@{utt}; @{tt}users/{id}@{utt}: @{tt}GET@{utt}, @{tt}PATCH@{utt}, @{tt}DELETE@{utt}, @{tt}POST activate@{utt}, @{tt}deactivate@{utt}, @{tt}promote@{utt}, @{tt}demote@{utt}), @{tt}GET active-sessions@{utt}, @{tt}POST active-sessions/{id}/cancel@{utt}, @{tt}GET system@{utt}, @{tt}GET security/audit@{utt}
@{tt}GET /api/ui/catalog@{utt}, @{tt}/ui/kit@{utt}, @{tt}/ui/panels/{name}@{utt} | the panel catalog and the core panels
@{tt}GET /api/help/node@{utt}, @{tt}/api/help/search@{utt}, @{tt}/api/help/asset@{utt} | this help
@{tt}/hooks@{utt}, @{tt}/hooks/{name}@{utt}, @{tt}/hooks/stats/...@{utt} | registered hooks and their statistics (admin)
@{tt}/tools/status@{utt}, @{tt}/tools/cache/...@{utt} | tool servers and the tool cache (admin)
@{tt}/debug/memory/...@{utt}, @{tt}/debug/profile/...@{utt} | profiling (admin, and only with the environment flags, see @{" Running the server " link running})
@{tt}GET /status/meta@{utt} | the metrics of the status event queues; with @{tt}AGENT_STATUS_REQUIRE_AUTH=1@{utt} it also wants the @{tt}X-Status-Token@{utt} header to equal @{tt}AGENT_STATUS_TOKEN@{utt}
@{tt}GET /health@{utt}, @{tt}/api/health@{utt}, @{tt}/api/version@{utt} | open without login
@{tt}/plugins/<instance>/...@{utt} | each plugin's own endpoints and @{tt}static/@{utt} files
@{body}

Every agent can also be called as a model, through the OpenAI Responses
and Chat Completions API: see @{" OpenAI-compatible API " link openai_api/main}.

The web UI talks to the server only over HTTP and Server-Sent Events; there
is no WebSocket.
@endnode


@node architecture "Architecture of the core"
@{h1}Architecture of the core

The core is the package @{tt}src/agent_system/@{utt}. Next to it, at the
root of the repository, lie @{tt}static/@{utt} (the shell's scripts and the
UI kit in @{tt}static/kit/@{utt}), @{tt}templates/@{utt} (the shell, the
login page, the core panels, the kit's templates), @{tt}config/@{utt} and
@{tt}schemas/@{utt}.

@{table}
Where | What
@{tt}app.py@{utt} | builds the FastAPI application (@{tt}build_app@{utt}) and includes its routers; @{tt}run()@{utt} serves it, behind @{tt}agent-api@{utt} (@{tt}own_console.py@{utt}: on Windows a console of its own first)
@{tt}runtime.py@{utt} | the composition root: turns the configuration into running servers -- discover, merge the configuration, build, register
@{tt}config/@{utt} | @{tt}models/@{utt} (the Pydantic models, root @{tt}AgentSystemConfig@{utt}), @{tt}settings.py@{utt} (@{tt}load_settings@{utt}, @{tt}get_tool_server_config@{utt}) with its steps beside it: @{tt}layers.py@{utt} (includes, the local layer), @{tt}environment.py@{utt} (secrets files, @{tt}${NAME}@{utt}), @{tt}merging.py@{utt}, @{tt}inheritance.py@{utt}
@{tt}plugins/@{utt} | discovery, manifests (@{tt}plugin.toml@{utt}), @{tt}schema.yaml@{utt} loading, the tool and web adapters, the schema router
@{tt}tools/@{utt} | tool server base classes (@{tt}SchemaBasedToolServer@{utt}), @{tt}ToolServerRegistry@{utt}, status lines, the tool cache
@{tt}servers/agent/@{utt} | the @{tt}Agent@{utt} (@{tt}server.py@{utt}; its responsibilities one mixin each in @{tt}mixins/@{utt}: @{tt}run_events@{utt} in @{tt}run.py@{utt}, the step loop in the package @{tt}llm_loop/@{utt}), prompt strategies, tool discovery and the allowlist, the components for hooks, tool execution, session tracking and status
@{tt}hooks/@{utt} | @{tt}HookType@{utt}, @{tt}SchemaBasedPluginHook@{utt}, @{tt}HookRegistry@{utt}
@{tt}llm/@{utt} | @{tt}ChatMessage@{utt} and the other models, the provider registry, @{tt}create_llm_from_profile@{utt} (@{tt}factory.py@{utt}), pricing, token counting
@{tt}services/@{utt} | session manager and service, agent service, configuration service and reload, the session archive, background jobs, system status
@{tt}core/@{utt} | cancellation tokens, the request context, session presence (the locks)
@{tt}skills/@{utt} | @{tt}SkillRegistry@{utt}, running a skill as a command
@{tt}auth/@{utt} | the user database, tokens and API keys, the route rules
@{tt}api/@{utt} | the routers: runs (@{tt}/run@{utt}, @{tt}/events@{utt}), agents, chat commands, pages and status (@{tt}*_routes.py@{utt}, sharing an @{tt}AppContext@{utt}), sessions, auth, admin and debug
@{tt}ui/@{utt} | the panel catalog, the core panel routes, this help
@{tt}chat_commands.py@{utt}, @{tt}plugin_commands.py@{utt} | the command catalogue both the terminal and the browser use
@{tt}agent_cli.py@{utt}, @{tt}agent_run.py@{utt}, @{tt}cli_utils/@{utt} | the command-line clients
@{tt}paths.py@{utt} | the project root and the data directory (@{tt}data_path@{utt})
@{body}

@{h2}How a chat message runs

@{number}The browser sends @{tt}POST /events@{utt} (with attachments:
@{tt}POST /run@{utt}) and reads the answer as Server-Sent Events.
@{number}The application picks the agent (the one named, else the default)
and, if one was chosen, an LLM profile, and calls the agent's
@{tt}run_events@{utt}.
@{number}The agent loads the session, renders its system prompt (template,
skills), works out its tools from the allowlist, and on a new session runs
the @{tt}session_start@{utt} hooks.
@{number}Each step: @{tt}pre_llm_call@{utt} hooks, the LLM call,
@{tt}post_llm_call@{utt} hooks, then the tool calls
(@{tt}pre_tool_call@{utt} hooks before each, @{tt}post_tool_call@{utt}
hooks on its result), with status events
streamed to the browser -- until the model answers without a tool call or
the step budget is spent.
@{number}The session is saved, then the @{tt}session_end@{utt} hooks run.

@{tt}agent-cli@{utt} runs the same agent code in its own process.

@{h2}What a plugin gets

A plugin is found by discovery, built by the runtime with
@{tt}(name, system_config, server_config)@{utt}, and reached through its
tools, its hooks and its web router. See
@{" Writing a plugin " link plugins}.
@endnode


@node plugins "Writing a plugin"
@{h1}Writing a plugin

A plugin is a folder with a @{tt}plugin.toml@{utt}, inside one of the
folders listed in @{tt}plugins.plugin_dirs@{utt}
(@{tt}config/plugins.yaml@{utt}; the product's own plugins are in
@{tt}src/plugins/@{utt}). @{b}The plugin type is the folder name@{ub}, not
the @{tt}name@{utt} in @{tt}plugin.toml@{utt}. Look first whether one
already exists: often a second server entry of an existing plugin with a
different configuration (@{tt}type: file_ops@{utt}) is all that is needed.
@{" Installed plugins " link plugins/main}.

@{h2}Which kind

What a plugin offers is detected from what it defines: tools from a
@{tt}tools:@{utt} section in @{tt}schema.yaml@{utt}, hooks from a
@{tt}hooks:@{utt} section, a panel from @{tt}get_web_router()@{utt}. The
@{tt}type@{utt} in @{tt}plugin.toml@{utt} is read by the validator -- and by
the LLM registry, which accepts only @{tt}llm-provider@{utt}.

@{table}
You want | type | Base class or shape | More
functions for the model | @{tt}tool-server@{utt} | @{tt}SchemaBasedToolServer@{utt} | @{" Tools " link tools}
to change LLM calls or the history | @{tt}hooks@{utt} | @{tt}SchemaBasedPluginHook@{utt} | @{" Hooks " link hooks}
both | @{tt}["tool-server","hooks"]@{utt} | @{tt}SchemaBasedHookToolServer@{utt} | @{" Hooks " link hooks}
a panel or endpoints | @{tt}web@{utt} | @{tt}get_web_router()@{utt} | @{" Panels " link panels}
an agent without Python | @{tt}library@{utt} | @{tt}agents/*.yaml@{utt}, @{tt}prompts/@{utt}, @{tt}skills/@{utt} | @{" Writing an agent " link agentdev}
an agent with code | @{tt}tool-server@{utt} | @{tt}SchemaBasedAgent@{utt} | @{" Writing an agent " link agentdev}
an LLM provider | @{tt}llm-provider@{utt} | @{tt}provider.py@{utt} exporting @{tt}PROVIDERS@{utt} | @{" LLM " link llm}
@{body}

@{h2}Minimal layout (tool server)

@{code text}
src/plugins/my_plugin/
  plugin.toml      # required
  plugin.py        # the entrypoint module, directly in the folder
  server.py
  schema.yaml      # tools; lies next to the class's module
  README.md        # a short overview
  my_plugin.guide  # the manual, shown in the Help panel
  tests/test_plugin_my_plugin_*.py
@{body}

@{code toml}
[plugin]
name = "my_plugin"
version = "0.1.0"
description = "One line."
entrypoint = "plugin:PLUGIN_FACTORY"   # the default; "server:MyServer" works too
type = ["tool-server"]
category = "tools"
requires = { agent_system = ">=0.7.0" }
dependencies = []                        # pip requirement strings
@{body}

@{code python}
# plugin.py
from .server import MyServer
PLUGIN_FACTORY = MyServer   # called as (name, system_config, server_config)
@{body}

A folder without the entrypoint file or without the factory is skipped with
a DEBUG log line only; an import error is a WARNING, and every server entry
of that type then logs "Unknown server type". After adding a pip dependency
run @{tt}python scripts/aggregate_plugin_deps.py@{utt}.

@{h2}The activation chain

Every link must hold, and almost every one fails @{b}silently@{ub}:

@{number}The folder lies in a directory of @{tt}plugins.plugin_dirs@{utt}.
@{number}A server entry @{tt}plugins: servers: <instance>: {type: <folder>,
enabled: true}@{utt} exists in an included file. @{tt}enabled@{utt}
defaults to @{b}false@{ub}.
@{number}The agent's @{tt}agent_config.tools.allowed@{utt} matches the tool
names, which carry the instance prefix (@{tt}my_plugin/*@{utt}). Empty
allows nothing.
@{number}For a sub-agent: it is enabled in the calling SAM, see
@{" Sub-agents and the SAM " link subagents}.

A new server entry needs a restart of the server.

@{h2}Reading configuration

@{tt}server_config@{utt} is a Pydantic model that allows extra keys, not a
dict:

@{code python}
self.timeout = float(getattr(server_config, "timeout", 30))   # flat keys
cfg = getattr(server_config, "config", None) or {}              # a nested config: block
@{body}

The framework does @{b}not@{ub} validate a plugin's own settings. For a
tool server the @{tt}config:@{utt} block in @{tt}schema.yaml@{utt} is
documentation; the defaults belong in the code. @{tt}${NAME}@{utt} values
are expanded from the environment. Files go under the data directory:
@{tt}data_path("my_plugin", "x.db")@{utt} from @{tt}agent_system.paths@{utt},
called at run time -- never a spelled-out @{tt}data/@{utt}. To take changes
without a restart, implement @{tt}reload_config(new_server_config)@{utt}
(with a @{tt}system_config@{utt} parameter as well, it gets the freshly
loaded system config too). A plugin that builds an LLM client keeps it in a
@{tt}ProfileClients@{utt} (@{tt}agent_system.llm.factory@{utt}): a reload
that changes the model behind the profile then reaches it.
See @{" Config models and schemas " link devconfig}.

@{h2}Checking it

@{code bash}
python src/scripts/validate_plugin.py src/plugins/my_plugin
python src/scripts/validate_all_tool_schemas.py --plugin my_plugin
python src/scripts/validate_agent_configs.py src/plugins/my_plugin/agents/*.yaml
@{body}

They check the manifest, the schema sections and that every tool routes to a
method -- but not the silent links of the activation chain (a dead allowlist
pattern, a sub-agent missing from the SAM). Check those in a test with
@{tt}load_settings()@{utt} and @{tt}get_tool_server_config()@{utt}, as
@{tt}src/plugins/research/tests/test_research_config.py@{utt} does.
@{tt}agent-cli plugins list@{utt} shows what was discovered and whether it
is enabled.

@{h2}Documentation

A plugin's @{tt}README.md@{utt} is a short overview; its manual is a
guide of its own, @{tt}<folder>/<folder>.guide@{utt}, which this help shows
instead of the README (@{" Writing a guide " link authoring}): the panel,
every tool and hook with its settings, and for a plugin whose tools a model
calls a @{b}Model Experience@{ub} part -- what the model sees (tool
descriptions, error texts, injected notes), the token and prompt-cache
effect, and known gaps. Until a plugin has a guide, that part is in its
README. Tests:
@{" Tests and conventions " link testing}.
@endnode


@node tools "Tools"
@{h1}Tools

A tool is one function a tool server offers the model, described by a JSON
schema the model reads as its contract. Good examples to copy from:
@{tt}src/plugins/json_store@{utt}, @{tt}sqlite_query@{utt},
@{tt}tool_script@{utt}, @{tt}todo@{utt}.

@{h2}The server

@{code python}
import asyncio
import logging
from agent_system.tools.schema_based import SchemaBasedToolServer

logger = logging.getLogger(__name__)   # there is no self.logger

class MyServer(SchemaBasedToolServer):
    def __init__(self, name, system_config, server_config):
        super().__init__(name, system_config, server_config)
        self.max_rows = int(getattr(server_config, "max_rows", 200))

    async def query(self, params: dict) -> dict:          # tool "{name}_query"
        status = params.get("_status")
        token = params.get("_cancellation_token")
        sql = params.get("sql")
        if not sql:
            if status: await status.error("sql parameter is required")
            return {"status": "error", "error": "sql parameter is required"}
        if token and token.is_cancelled:
            return {"error": "Tool 'query' was cancelled.", "cancelled": True, "forced": False}
        rows = await asyncio.to_thread(self._run, sql)    # never block the event loop
        rows = rows[: self.max_rows]
        if status: await status.end(f"Query returned {len(rows)} row(s)")
        return {"status": "success", "rows": rows}
@{body}

@{h2}schema.yaml

@{code yaml}
tools:
  - type: function
    function:
      name: "{{ name }}_query"
      description: |
        What it does, when to use it, what it returns, how to recover.
      parameters:
        type: object
        properties:
          sql: {type: string, description: "..."}
        required: ["sql"]
        additionalProperties: false
commands: []    # optional: slash commands, below
web_ui: {}      # optional: a panel
@{body}

@{bullet}The file is rendered as @{b}Jinja first@{ub}, then parsed as YAML.
The one default variable is @{tt}{{ name }}@{utt}, the instance name. A
variable you forgot renders as an empty string, silently.
@{bullet}@{b}Routing@{ub}: the tool @{tt}{name}_x@{utt} calls the method
@{tt}x@{utt}; a tool named exactly @{tt}{name}@{utt} calls
@{tt}execute@{utt}; anything else calls the method of the same name.
@{bullet}@{b}Always prefix @{tt}{{ name }}_@{utt}@{ub}: two tools with the
same name collide silently, and a second instance of the plugin needs names
of its own.
@{bullet}Parameter names never start with @{tt}_@{utt} and are never
@{tt}request_id@{utt}: the framework sets those and drops what the model
sends.
@{bullet}A broken @{tt}schema.yaml@{utt} does not fail in
@{tt}__init__@{utt}, only on the first @{tt}get_tools()@{utt} -- test that
path.

@{h2}The handler contract

@{bullet}@{tt}async def tool(self, params: dict) -> dict@{utt}. The
framework adds @{tt}_status@{utt}, @{tt}_request_id@{utt},
@{tt}_session_id@{utt}, @{tt}_user_id@{utt}, @{tt}_agent_name@{utt},
@{tt}_agent@{utt} and @{tt}_cancellation_token@{utt} -- the token only when
there is a request id (not in tests, not for commands).
@{bullet}@{b}No argument validation by the framework@{ub} beyond rejecting
malformed JSON; @{tt}required@{utt} and @{tt}enum@{utt} are hints to the
model. Check inputs yourself.
@{bullet}Return @{tt}{"status": "success", ...}@{utt} or
@{tt}{"status": "error", "error": "<what to do>"}@{utt}. A dict that looks
like success on a failure is a silent failure; @{tt}{"status": "failed"}@{utt}
or @{tt}{"success": false}@{utt} without @{tt}error@{utt} are shown as
completed.
@{bullet}The result reaches the model as JSON. Bound its size yourself
(@{tt}max_rows@{utt}, paging): only the @{tt}context_engineer@{utt} hook caps
tool results, and only for agents that run it.

@{h2}Status and cancellation

@{tt}_status@{utt} has exactly @{tt}progress(msg)@{utt}, @{tt}end(msg)@{utt}
and @{tt}error(msg)@{utt}. Report steps with @{tt}progress@{utt}, then
exactly one @{tt}end@{utt} or @{tt}error@{utt} whose text names the result (a
count, ids, a title) in at most 140 characters -- the web UI shows only that
line. @{tt}tests/plugins/test_status_end_lines.py@{utt} enforces it for every
plugin.

The cancellation token can be @{tt}None@{utt}; check
@{tt}if token and token.is_cancelled@{utt} before starting and between
chunks, and put blocking work into @{tt}asyncio.to_thread@{utt}. Return the
cancelled dict shown above rather than raising. A user cancels with the
@{b}Stop@{ub} button, Ctrl-C in @{tt}agent-cli chat@{utt}, or
@{tt}POST /api/requests/{id}/cancel@{utt}.

@{h2}Slash commands

@{code yaml}
commands:
  - name: compact
    description: "Compact the context now"
    tool: "{{ name }}_compact"
    argument: focus          # optional: the rest of the line goes into this parameter
    argument_hint: "<topic>" # optional: shown in /help
    params: {mode: now}      # optional: fixed for every call
@{body}

@{tt}params@{utt} is what makes one operation of a multi-operation tool a
command of its own (@{tt}{operation: list}@{utt}); it may not fix the
parameter @{tt}argument@{utt} binds. Neither may name a parameter that
starts with @{tt}_@{utt} or is a request id, session id or agent name. A
malformed entry is dropped with a warning in the log.

A command runs the tool directly, without an LLM turn, through the agent's
allowlist: if the current agent may not call the tool, the command is not
offered. On a name clash with another plugin it is
@{tt}/<plugin>:name@{utt}. See @{" Chat commands " link commands}.
@endnode


@node hooks "Hooks"
@{h1}Hooks

A hook is plugin code that runs at a fixed point of the agent loop and may
change what happens next. The types (@{tt}HookType@{utt} in
@{tt}src/agent_system/hooks/plugin_hook.py@{utt}):

@{table}
Type | Fires | What takes effect
@{tt}session_start@{utt} | new session only, before history and user input | @{tt}messages@{utt}
@{tt}pre_llm_call@{utt} | every step, before the LLM call | @{tt}messages@{utt} -- sent as changed, and kept in the session
@{tt}llm_progress@{utt} | while a reply streams, every 2000 characters of thinking | nothing
@{tt}post_llm_call@{utt} | after the assistant message is appended | its content and tool calls; @{tt}continue@{utt} in the metadata asks for another step
@{tt}pre_tool_call@{utt} | before each tool call of the model, and of a @{tt}tool_script@{utt} script | @{tt}tool_call["arguments"]@{utt}; @{tt}metadata["block"]@{utt} (a text, or true) stops the call -- the model reads the text as its result and the run goes on
@{tt}post_tool_call@{utt} | after the calls of a step ran, before their results join the history (for a script: after its call) | @{tt}tool_result["result"]@{utt}
@{tt}session_end@{utt} | after the session is saved | nothing
@{tt}pre_llm_request@{utt}, @{tt}post_llm_response@{utt} | in the LLM client: the exact request and response | read-only
@{body}

The first @{tt}pre_tool_call@{utt} hook that blocks ends the chain. A
@{tt}pre_tool_call@{utt} hook that fails lets the call run, unless its
schema entry says @{tt}on_error: block@{utt} -- for a hook that exists to
stop calls, such as @{" tool_approval " link tool_approval/main}.

@{h2}A minimal hook plugin

@{tt}plugin.toml@{utt}: @{tt}type = ["hooks"]@{utt},
@{tt}category = "hooks"@{utt}.

@{code yaml}
# schema.yaml
hooks:
  - name: inject_note          # the method's name
    type: pre_llm_call
    enabled: false             # usually off; an agent switches it on
    timeout: 5.0               # default: hooks.default_timeout
    category: prompt_injection
    order: {after: ["begin"], before: ["end"]}
config:
  note: {type: string, default: ""}
@{body}

@{code python}
# hooks.py
from agent_system.hooks import SchemaBasedPluginHook, HookContext, HookResult
from agent_system.llm.message_roles import DEVELOPER
from agent_system.llm.models import ChatMessage

MARK = "my_hook"

class MyHook(SchemaBasedPluginHook):
    def __init__(self, plugin_dir, server_config=None):
        super().__init__(plugin_dir)          # the schema.yaml defaults
        cfg = dict(self.get_config())
        if server_config is not None and getattr(server_config, "config", None):
            cfg.update(server_config.config)  # merging the entry's config is the plugin's job
        self.note = cfg.get("note", "")

    async def inject_note(self, context: HookContext) -> HookResult:
        note = context.hook_config.get("note", self.note)   # per-agent keys
        if not note or context.messages is None:
            return HookResult(success=True, modified=False, context=context)
        # appended at the end, never inserted at the front (prompt cache, below)
        context.messages.append(ChatMessage(role=DEVELOPER, content=note, injected_by=MARK))
        return HookResult(success=True, modified=True, context=context)
@{body}

@{code python}
# plugin.py
from pathlib import Path
from .hooks import MyHook

def PLUGIN_FACTORY(name=None, system_config=None, server_config=None):
    return MyHook(Path(__file__).parent, server_config)
@{body}

Each hook gets its own copy of the messages: changes count only with
@{tt}modified=True@{utt}, and @{tt}success=False@{utt} drops them. A hook
that raises, times out or returns something invalid is logged and skipped,
and the loop goes on with the unchanged messages -- @{b}a broken hook shows
only in the log@{ub}.

@{h2}Names, order, switching on and off

@{bullet}The @{b}full name@{ub} is @{tt}<server instance>.<hook name>@{utt},
not the plugin type.
@{bullet}@{tt}order@{utt} understands full names, categories,
@{tt}begin@{utt} and @{tt}end@{utt}; a short name is ignored without a word.
@{bullet}Globally: the @{tt}hooks:@{utt} section (shipped in
@{tt}config/plugins.yaml@{utt}) has @{tt}enabled@{utt} (the master switch),
@{tt}default_timeout@{utt} and @{tt}overrides@{utt}, keyed by
@{tt}<instance>.<hook>@{utt} or by @{tt}<instance>@{utt} for all its hooks.
@{bullet}Per agent: @{tt}agent_config.hooks.enabled: false@{utt} switches
all hooks of the agent off;
@{tt}agent_config.hooks.overrides["<full name>"].enabled@{utt} switches one;
every other key reaches the hook as @{tt}context.hook_config@{utt}. A key
that is not a full hook name has no effect; the start logs a warning for it.
@{bullet}A hook plugin runs only when its instance is
@{tt}enabled: true@{utt}.
@{bullet}Hook settings are wired at start: changing them needs a restart.

@{h2}The rule that matters for cost

Providers cache the beginning of a request. @{b}Every LLM request must be a
prefix of the next one.@{ub} A hook or prompt that puts something changing (a
clock, a counter) into the system prompt or at the front of the history, or
rewrites an earlier message, pays for the whole context again on every step.
Append at the end; find your own earlier insert by @{tt}injected_by@{utt}
and replace it rather than adding another. Every @{tt}role: user@{utt}
message a hook inserts carries @{tt}injected_by@{utt} -- a message without
it counts as written by a person.

Inspect: @{tt}agent-cli hooks list@{utt}, @{tt}agent-cli hooks inspect
NAME@{utt}, or @{tt}GET /hooks@{utt} and @{tt}GET /hooks/{name}@{utt}
(admin).
@endnode


@node panels "Panels and the UI kit"
@{h1}Panels and the UI kit

A panel is an HTML page the shell shows in a frame, docked as a tab or
floating as a window, opened through the catalog
(@{tt}GET /api/ui/catalog@{utt}). The same page also works on its own in a
browser tab. The frame is a boundary for style, crashes and lifecycle --
@{b}not for security@{ub}: panels run on the same origin with the shell's
cookie. Never embed foreign code as a panel.

The @{b}UI Kit@{ub} panel (@{tt}/ui/kit@{utt}) shows every component in
every state, rendered from the same files a panel loads. Look there instead
of copying markup from older panels.

@{h2}1. The catalog entry (schema.yaml)

@{code yaml}
web_ui:
  panel:
    endpoint: "/plugins/{{ name }}/"
    title: "Message Debugger"
    description: "Captured message turns and raw API requests and responses"
    icon: bug
    category: debug
    keywords: [requests, responses, llm]
    window: {width: 1000, height: 700}
    contexts:
      request: "/plugins/{{ name }}/?request_id={request_id}"
  endpoints:
    - path: "/"
      method: "GET"
      handler: "render_panel"
      response_type: "html"
@{body}

@{bullet}Required: @{tt}endpoint@{utt}, @{tt}title@{utt}, @{tt}icon@{utt}
(an id from @{tt}static/kit/icons.svg@{utt}) and @{tt}category@{utt} (one of
the fixed list in @{tt}src/agent_system/ui/catalog.py@{utt}, such as
@{tt}session@{utt}, @{tt}context@{utt}, @{tt}agents@{utt},
@{tt}debug@{utt}, @{tt}system@{utt}, @{tt}admin@{utt}).
@{bullet}@{tt}contexts@{utt}: entry points from the chat --
@{tt}session@{utt} (a session's panel button, the session title) and
@{tt}request@{utt} (the request id under an answer). The URL must start
with @{tt}endpoint@{utt}; the shell fills in @{tt}{session_id}@{utt} or
@{tt}{request_id}@{utt}. A @{tt}session@{utt} context @{b}pins@{ub} a
panel that otherwise follows the chat, so show the pinned session visibly
(@{tt}<pk-session>@{utt} in the toolbar). With @{tt}session@{utt} give the
plugin object @{tt}has_session_data(session_id, user_id)@{utt} too (cheap,
sync or async): the session menu then offers the panel only where it holds
data; without it the panel stands in every session's menu. A sync method
runs in a worker thread, an async one on the event loop, where it must not
block.
@{bullet}@{tt}web_ui@{utt} knows exactly @{tt}panel@{utt} and
@{tt}endpoints@{utt}; anything else is an error.
@{bullet}@{b}Who sees the panel is not in this block@{ub}: the catalog
offers it to the roles that both @{tt}auth.endpoint_security@{utt} and
@{tt}auth.plugin_security@{utt} let through to its @{tt}endpoint@{utt}. An
admin-only panel gets a rule in the configuration, and visibility and access
match by themselves.
@{bullet}The plugin must provide @{tt}get_web_router()@{utt} @{b}and@{ub}
@{tt}get_schema_data()@{utt}; schema-based plugins inherit both from
@{tt}SchemaBasedPluginWebInterface@{utt} and build the router from
@{tt}web_ui.endpoints@{utt} with @{tt}SchemaRouterGenerator@{utt}. Without
@{tt}get_schema_data()@{utt} the panel is missing, without a message.

@{h2}2. The page

@{code python}
from agent_system.ui.resources import ui_templates

templates = ui_templates(Path(__file__).parent / "templates")

async def render_panel(self, request: Request):
    return templates.TemplateResponse(request, "panel.html", {"plugin": self.name})
@{body}

@{code html}
{% extends "kit/panel_base.html" %}
{% block title %}Message Debugger{% endblock %}
{% block toolbar %}<pk-refresh interval="5"></pk-refresh>{% endblock %}
{% block content %}<div id="turns"></div>{% endblock %}
{% block scripts %}<script type="module" src="/plugins/{{ plugin }}/static/panel.js"></script>{% endblock %}
@{body}

@{tt}panel_base.html@{utt} sets the theme before the page paints, loads
@{tt}kit.css@{utt} and @{tt}panel-kit.js@{utt}, and hides its own title
inside the shell. Script and CSS lie in the plugin's @{tt}static/@{utt}
folder, served at @{tt}/plugins/<name>/static/@{utt} when the plugin returns
it from @{tt}get_static_assets()@{utt}.

@{h2}3. panel-kit.js

@{code javascript}
import { api, html, render, session } from '/static/kit/panel-kit.js';

async function load() {
  const turns = await api(`/plugins/message_debugger/turns?session_id=${encodeURIComponent(session.id)}`);
  render(document.getElementById('turns'),
    html`<table class="pk-table">${turns.map((t) => html`<tr><td>${t.agent}</td></tr>`)}</table>`);
}
document.addEventListener('refresh', load);
session.onChange(load);
load();
@{body}

@{table}
Export | For
@{tt}api(path, {method, json, quiet, latest})@{utt} | every server call: cookie login, JSON in and out, errors as a toast (unless @{tt}quiet@{utt}); @{tt}latest: 'name'@{utt} aborts the previous call of that name
@{tt}html`...`@{utt}, @{tt}render(el, x)@{utt}, @{tt}update(el, x)@{utt} | building markup: every inserted value is escaped; @{tt}update@{utt} redraws only when the markup changed
@{tt}alert@{utt}, @{tt}confirm@{utt}, @{tt}prompt@{utt}, @{tt}dialog@{utt}, @{tt}toast@{utt} | dialogs and messages, drawn over the whole shell
@{tt}session.id@{utt}, @{tt}session.onChange(fn)@{utt}, @{tt}<pk-session>@{utt} | the session open in the chat, and the one the panel shows
@{tt}<pk-refresh interval="s">@{utt}, @{tt}autoRefresh(fn, ms)@{utt} | reloading, paused while the panel is not visible
@{tt}notice@{utt}, @{tt}emptyState@{utt}, @{tt}skeleton@{utt}, @{tt}localTime@{utt}, @{tt}copyText@{utt} | messages in place, empty and loading states, time stamps, the clipboard
@{tt}setDirty(bool)@{utt} | unsaved input: the shell asks before closing
@{body}

@{h2}4. Rules

@{bullet}Components and @{b}tokens only@{ub}: @{tt}pk-btn@{utt},
@{tt}pk-table@{utt}, @{tt}pk-tabs@{utt}, @{tt}pk-callout@{utt} and the rest;
colours, spacing and fonts from the CSS tokens (@{tt}--surface-*@{utt},
@{tt}--text-*@{utt}, @{tt}--space-*@{utt}, ...), valid in light and dark. A
hex colour in a panel is a mistake.
@{bullet}No native @{tt}alert@{utt}/@{tt}confirm@{utt}/@{tt}prompt@{utt}, no
@{tt}innerHTML@{utt} with data (agent names and tool arguments come from
models), no @{tt}window.parent@{utt} or @{tt}window.top@{utt}, no raw
@{tt}fetch@{utt}, no buttons, tabs, modals or toasts of your own, no emoji as
icons, no CDN (libraries go to @{tt}static/vendor/@{utt}).

@{h2}5. Checking it

@{tt}python src/scripts/validate_plugin.py src/plugins/<name>@{utt} runs the
@{tt}web_ui@{utt} block through the catalog parser; @{tt}pytest tests/ui@{utt}
checks icons, native dialogs and catalog rules. Then look at the panel in
both themes, docked and floating, and once on its own in a browser tab.

A panel can also show its plugin's guide in place with
@{tt}<pk-guide>@{utt}, see @{" Writing a guide " link authoring}.
@endnode


@node agentdev "Writing an agent"
@{h1}Writing an agent

An agent is almost always pure configuration: a server entry with
@{tt}type: basic_agent@{utt}, or with the name of another agent it inherits
from. It lives in a YAML file under @{tt}config/agents*/@{utt} or in a
plugin's own @{tt}agents/@{utt} folder; @{tt}config/config.yaml@{utt}
includes both (@{tt}agents*/*.yaml@{utt} and
@{tt}../src/plugins*/*/agents/*.yaml@{utt}).

@{code yaml}
plugins:
  servers:
    my_agent:
      type: basic_agent
      enabled: true                  # default false
      description: "One line for humans and the SAM list."
      metadata:
        visibility: tool             # ui | tool | both | private (default)
      agent_config:                  # unknown keys here fail the load
        llm_profile: [primary, fallback]
        max_steps: 30
        system_template: "./prompts/my_agent.md"
        template_vars: {}
        skills: {always: [], on_demand: []}
        tools:
          allowed: ["file_ops/*", "my_sam/*"]
          blocked: []
          deferred: []               # schemas sent only once loaded
        hooks:
          overrides:
            context_engineer.engineer_context: {enabled: true}
@{body}

@{h2}Rules that bite

@{bullet}@{b}enabled defaults to false@{ub} and is read from the entry
itself, not from what it inherits. A top-level @{tt}agents:@{utt} key is
ignored, and a YAML file that does not parse is skipped as a whole.
@{bullet}@{b}Visibility@{ub}: @{tt}ui@{utt} or @{tt}both@{utt} puts the
agent into the web UI's picker; @{tt}tool@{utt} or @{tt}both@{utt} lets
other agents start it as a sub-agent. @{tt}private@{utt}, the default, is
neither.
@{bullet}@{b}Tools allowlist@{ub}: empty means nothing is allowed. Patterns
are fnmatch: @{tt}instance/*@{utt}, @{tt}instance@{utt}, or
@{tt}instance/<full tool name>@{utt}. A tool name always carries the plugin
instance's name as a prefix: the @{tt}semantic_search@{utt} tool of an
instance @{tt}coder_fs@{utt} is
@{tt}coder_fs/coder_fs_semantic_search@{utt};
@{tt}coder_fs/semantic_search@{utt} silently matches nothing. A second
instance of a plugin has its own tool names and needs its own entry. Tools
of external MCP servers are written @{tt}server.*@{utt}, see
@{" External MCP servers " link mcp}.
@{bullet}@{b}Deferred tools@{ub}: @{tt}tools.deferred@{utt} takes the same
patterns and holds the schemas of those allowed tools back. The model sees
them as a name and one line in the core tool @{tt}tool_search@{utt} and
loads what it needs; a call before the load is not run. Worth it for rare
tools with large schemas, not for ones nearly every run uses. Details in
@{tt}docs/deferred_tools.md@{utt}.
@{bullet}@{b}Inherited lists@{ub}: a list without prefixes replaces the
inherited one; @{tt}+item@{utt} adds to it, @{tt}!pattern@{utt} removes
from it; mixing both forms is an error.
@{bullet}@{b}Inheritance is invisible in the raw entry@{ub}:
@{tt}type:@{utt} may name another entry, and its configuration is merged
in. The raw @{tt}config.plugins.servers[name]@{utt} shows defaults, not the
inherited values; code asks @{tt}get_tool_server_config(name)@{utt}. What
an agent finally sees: @{tt}GET /agents/debug/{name}/system-prompt@{utt}
and @{tt}GET /agents/debug/{name}/allowed-tools@{utt} (admin).

@{h2}The system prompt

@{bullet}@{tt}system_template@{utt} with @{tt}./@{utt} or @{tt}../@{utt}
is relative to the YAML file; only @{tt}.md@{utt}, @{tt}.txt@{utt} and
@{tt}.markdown@{utt}. It is a Jinja template; @{tt}<!-- -->@{utt} comments
are removed first. @{tt}default_config@{utt} in
@{tt}config/plugins.yaml@{utt} gives every agent
@{tt}config/prompts/system_prompt.md@{utt}.
@{bullet}@{b}Precedence@{ub}: a custom prompt, then a raw
@{tt}system_prompt@{utt}, then @{tt}system_template@{utt}, then the
default. An inherited @{tt}system_prompt@{utt} silently beats your template
-- check the parent when an edit shows no effect.
@{bullet}Templates are @{b}read from disk on every render@{ub} (run start
and every step): an edit takes effect without a restart, even in a run under
way.
@{bullet}Nothing that changes on every call (a clock, a counter) belongs in
the prompt: it breaks the provider's prompt cache, see
@{" Hooks " link hooks}. The date comes from
@{tt}context.auto_datetime@{utt} in @{tt}config/config.yaml@{utt}.
@{bullet}A template can branch on what is installed:
@{tt}has_tool('*_manage_sub_agent')@{utt} (the agent's own tools),
@{tt}'some_plugin' in plugins@{utt}, @{tt}'github' in mcp_servers@{utt}.

@{h2}LLM profiles

@{tt}llm_profile@{utt} is a chain: the primary profile, then fallbacks.
@{tt}llm_profile_advanced@{utt} is a second chain for harder work. On
failure the agent tries the rest of its own chain, then the other chain.
@{tt}llm_profile: []@{utt} is an error. See
@{" LLM models and profiles " link llm}.

@{table}
Key | Default | Meaning
@{tt}llm_params@{utt} | none | model parameters laid over the chain's model entries: flat (@{tt}{max_tokens: 8000}@{utt}, for every member) or keyed by profile (@{tt}"*"@{utt} for all, a profile name for one). Which model it is (@{tt}provider@{utt}, @{tt}model@{utt}, @{tt}api_key@{utt}, @{tt}base_url@{utt}) cannot be set here; an unknown key fails the load
@{tt}inherit_parent_llm@{utt} | @{tt}false@{utt} | true: when the run that starts this agent was switched to another profile (the model picker, @{tt}--llm@{utt}, @{tt}/model@{utt}, the advanced model), this agent runs on that profile too; its own chain stays the fallback
@{tt}auto_escalate_on_stuck@{utt} | @{tt}false@{utt} | true: when the run is stuck -- a tool-call loop, or @{tt}escalate_error_streak@{utt} (2) steps in a row whose tool calls all failed -- switch to @{tt}llm_profile_advanced@{utt} for @{tt}escalate_rounds@{utt} (2) steps, at most @{tt}escalate_max_calls@{utt} (6) advanced calls per run
@{tt}fallback_recovery_seconds@{utt} | @{tt}3600@{utt} | the longest a failing model is set aside: a rate limit starts at 60 s and doubles up to this; a refused key (401, 402, 403, 404) or an exhausted quota blocks this long at once
@{tt}network_outage_seconds@{utt} | @{tt}1800@{utt} | when no model of the chain could be reached at all (the connect timed out or found no route, a name that does not resolve; a refused connection ends the run at once): the step waits, 10 s doubling up to 2 min, and asks the run's own model again, for this many seconds from the start of the outage; 0: the run ends at once
@{tt}loop_detection@{utt} | on | repeated identical tool calls: a warning after @{tt}exact_match_threshold@{utt} (3) of them or @{tt}sequence_threshold@{utt} (2) repeated sequences, the tool blocked after @{tt}block_after_threshold@{utt} (5), released after @{tt}auto_unblock_after_steps@{utt} (3) steps
@{tt}reasoning_loop@{utt} | on | a call whose thinking keeps repeating itself is aborted and the same model tried once more (streaming calls only; @{tt}repetition_threshold@{utt} 0.5)
@{tt}output_cap_notes@{utt} | @{tt}0@{utt} | how often in a row an answer cut off at the output limit is sent back with a note instead of delivered; for agents that work through tool calls
@{tt}timeouts@{utt} | see @{tt}config/plugins.yaml@{utt} | internal safety timeouts (status queue, session lock, tool clean-up)
@{body}

@{tt}metadata.min_role@{utt} limits who may run the agent, see
@{" Users, roles and login " link auth}. Asking a person before a tool
runs is the @{" tool_approval " link tool_approval/main} hook, switched on
per agent.

@{h2}Reload or restart

@{tt}agent-cli reload@{utt} gives a running agent its whole new
@{tt}agent_config@{utt} (model chain, LLM params, tools, hooks, timeouts,
prompt) and its description and metadata; a model changed in
@{tt}llm.yaml@{utt} reaches every agent on that profile. A new agent needs
a restart. Prompt templates need neither. See
@{" Running the server " link running}.

@{h2}An agent with code

Rarely needed. Subclass @{tt}SchemaBasedAgent@{utt}
(@{tt}agent_system.servers.agent.schema_based@{utt}) and export
@{tt}PLUGIN_FACTORY = make_agent_plugin_factory(MyAgent)@{utt}
(@{tt}agent_system.plugins.factory_utils@{utt}) -- always through that
factory, or the agent gets an empty private registry. Its methods become
tools the same way as in any @{" tool server " link tools}.

To make it a sub-agent of another agent, see
@{" Sub-agents and the SAM " link subagents}.
@endnode


@node skills "Skills"
@{h1}Skills

A skill packages reusable agent knowledge as a directory with a
@{tt}SKILL.md@{utt} -- YAML frontmatter with @{tt}name@{utt} and
@{tt}description@{utt}, then the instructions -- and optional
@{tt}references/@{utt}, @{tt}scripts/@{utt} and @{tt}assets/@{utt} folders.
This is the Agent Skills layout other coding assistants read too, so a skill
written for one of them works here. The body is used as written, not
rendered as a template.

@{b}Where skills are found@{ub}: every subfolder with a @{tt}SKILL.md@{utt}
in a root listed in @{tt}skills.skill_dirs@{utt} of
@{tt}config/config.yaml@{utt}. The shipped list is
@{tt}.claude/skills@{utt}, @{tt}skills/*/@{utt} (grouped as
@{tt}skills/<group>/<skill>/@{utt}) and @{tt}src/plugins*/*/skills@{utt}
(the skills a plugin ships). Without the key, the roots are those in the
environment variable @{tt}AGENT_SKILL_DIRS@{utt} (separated like
@{tt}PATH@{utt}), else @{tt}skills@{utt} and @{tt}.claude/skills@{utt}. A
list replaces those defaults entirely. The first root that defines a name
wins.

@{b}Being found puts nothing into a prompt.@{ub} Only an agent's own
configuration does that:

@{code yaml}
agent_config:
  skills:
    always: [some-skill]        # the whole body, in every render
    on_demand: [other-skill]    # a one-line entry; the agent loads it when needed
@{body}

A skill an agent names but that cannot be found is logged as an error when
the prompt renders, and the prompt renders without it.

@{b}From the chat@{ub}: every discovered skill can be run as
@{tt}/skill-name arguments@{utt}; its text becomes the message.
@{tt}/skills@{utt} lists the runnable ones and what the current agent loads.
A built-in command or a plugin command of the same name comes first, see
@{" Chat commands " link commands}.
@endnode


@node subagents "Sub-agents and the SAM"
@{h1}Sub-agents and the SAM

A sub-agent is an ordinary agent that another agent starts and exchanges
messages with through a tool, keeping its own session and history across
calls. The @{b}sub_agent_manager@{ub} plugin (SAM) does this. An instance of
it offers one tool, @{tt}<instance>_manage_sub_agent@{utt}, with the
operations @{tt}create@{utt}, @{tt}continue@{utt}, @{tt}poll@{utt},
@{tt}wait@{utt}, @{tt}wait_all@{utt}, @{tt}cancel@{utt}, @{tt}list@{utt},
@{tt}info@{utt} and @{tt}delete@{utt}. There is no global list of
sub-agents: each SAM instance decides which agents its callers may start.

@{h2}Four conditions, none of them loud

A sub-agent that should be startable but is not almost always misses one of
these:

@{number}@{b}It exists@{ub}: its YAML is included, @{tt}enabled: true@{utt},
and its @{tt}type@{utt} resolves. Otherwise:
@{tt}Agent type 'x' not found in registry@{utt}.
@{number}@{b}The calling SAM allows it@{ub}: its instance name is in that
SAM instance's @{tt}allowed_agents@{utt} (exact names or fnmatch patterns;
without the key: @{tt}['*']@{utt}). @{tt}blocked_agents@{utt} is checked
first and matches exact names only. Otherwise the call answers
@{tt}agent_blocked@{utt}.
@{number}@{b}The caller may use the SAM@{ub}:
@{tt}tools.allowed: ["my_sam/*"]@{utt} in the calling agent.
@{number}@{b}It is visible@{ub}: @{tt}metadata.visibility@{utt} is
@{tt}tool@{utt} or @{tt}both@{utt}. The default, @{tt}private@{utt}, leaves
it out of the SAM's list of available agents, so the model never learns its
name.

@{h2}SAM settings

The SAM's knobs are @{b}top-level keys of its server entry@{ub}, not under a
@{tt}config:@{utt} block (that block in the SAM's @{tt}schema.yaml@{utt} is
not read): @{tt}allowed_agents@{utt}, @{tt}blocked_agents@{utt},
@{tt}allow_advanced_model@{utt} (default true; false drops a caller's
request for the advanced model), @{tt}advanced_create_only_agents@{utt},
@{tt}advanced_continue_only_agents@{utt}, and
the limits @{tt}max_sub_agents_per_session@{utt} (10),
@{tt}max_nesting_depth@{utt} (5) and @{tt}max_sub_agents_per_type@{utt}
(3). Everything else -- operations, waiting, background runs, the map
panel: the @{" Sub-Agent Manager " link sub_agent_manager/main} guide.

@{code yaml}
plugins:
  servers:
    my_sam:
      type: sub_agent_manager
      enabled: true
      allowed_agents: [my_agent, "review_*"]
      blocked_agents: []
@{body}

These settings and the limits take effect with @{tt}agent-cli reload@{utt}.
A @{b}new@{ub} sub-agent needs a restart, because its server has to be
built. Writing the agent itself: @{" Writing an agent " link agentdev}.
@endnode


@node devconfig "Config models and schemas"
@{h1}Config models and schemas

@{h2}The models

The whole configuration is validated by Pydantic models in
@{tt}src/agent_system/config/models/@{utt}, a module per part of the configuration. The root is
@{tt}AgentSystemConfig@{utt} with the sections @{tt}name@{utt},
@{tt}version@{utt}, @{tt}description@{utt}, @{tt}includes@{utt},
@{tt}paths@{utt}, @{tt}context@{utt}, @{tt}status@{utt}, @{tt}vision@{utt},
@{tt}session_presence@{utt}, @{tt}session_archive@{utt},
@{tt}network@{utt}, @{tt}default_agent@{utt}, @{tt}logging@{utt},
@{tt}auth@{utt}, @{tt}skills@{utt}, @{tt}llm_system@{utt},
@{tt}plugins@{utt}, @{tt}external_servers@{utt} and @{tt}hooks@{utt}.

@{bullet}A server entry (@{tt}ToolServerConfig@{utt}) allows extra keys:
that is where a plugin's own settings go, unchecked.
@{bullet}@{tt}agent_config@{utt} forbids unknown keys: a typo there fails
the load, with its name.
@{bullet}@{b}When you change a model@{ub}, regenerate the JSON schemas in
@{tt}schemas/@{utt} with @{tt}python src/scripts/generate_config_schemas.py@{utt};
@{tt}tests/config/test_config_schemas.py@{utt} fails while they differ.

@{h2}Loading

@{bullet}@{tt}load_settings(path)@{utt} in
@{tt}src/agent_system/config/settings.py@{utt} reads
@{tt}config.yaml@{utt} and its includes, expands @{tt}${NAME}@{utt} and
builds the models (see @{" Configuration files " link config} for the merge
rules).
@{bullet}@{tt}get_tool_server_config(name)@{utt} gives an entry as the
runtime sees it: @{tt}plugins.default_config@{utt} and the @{tt}type:@{utt}
chain merged in. The raw @{tt}config.plugins.servers[name]@{utt} does not
show that inheritance.
@{bullet}Inherited lists: no prefix replaces, @{tt}+item@{utt} adds,
@{tt}!pattern@{utt} removes; mixing both forms raises an error.

@{h2}Paths

The data directory can move (@{tt}AGENT_DATA_DIR@{utt},
@{tt}paths.data_dir@{utt}), so never spell out @{tt}data/@{utt}:

@{bullet}@{tt}data_path("my_plugin", "x.db")@{utt} from
@{tt}agent_system.paths@{utt}, called at run time, for a default;
@{bullet}@{tt}resolve_data_path(value)@{utt} for a value that comes from
the environment, a command line or a database row;
@{bullet}values under @{tt}data/@{utt} in @{tt}plugins.yaml@{utt} and in
@{tt}schema.yaml@{utt} defaults are moved by the loader.

@{tt}tests/config/test_no_hardcoded_data_dir.py@{utt} guards this.

@{h2}Live reload

A plugin that implements @{tt}reload_config(new_server_config)@{utt} gets
its fresh entry on @{tt}agent-cli reload@{utt} and returns what it changed;
everything else keeps its start values until a restart. See
@{" Running the server " link running}.
@endnode


@node testing "Tests and conventions"
@{h1}Tests and conventions

@{h2}Where tests live

@{bullet}@{tt}tests/@{utt}, by area (@{tt}agent/@{utt}, @{tt}config/@{utt},
@{tt}llm/@{utt}, @{tt}tools/@{utt}, @{tt}session/@{utt}, @{tt}hooks/@{utt},
@{tt}ui/@{utt}, @{tt}integration/@{utt}, ...).
@{bullet}A plugin's tests lie next to it:
@{tt}src/plugins/<name>/tests/test_plugin_<name>_*.py@{utt};
@{tt}tests/plugins/@{utt} is for tests across plugins. Test file names are
unique in the whole repository.

@{h2}How pytest is set up

@{bullet}@{tt}pytest.ini@{utt}: warnings are errors, @{tt}asyncio_mode = auto@{utt},
120 seconds per test, @{tt}pythonpath = src tests@{utt}.
@{bullet}The root @{tt}conftest.py@{utt} replaces the construction of LLM
clients with a fake: tests make no real LLM calls.
@{bullet}At the end of a run the root @{tt}conftest.py@{utt} kills leftover
Python processes it takes for this repository's. @{b}Do not run the tests
on a machine that serves users@{ub}: a running @{tt}agent-api@{utt} can be
among them.
@{bullet}The whole suite takes long: run what you changed, for example
@{tt}python -m pytest tests/ui/test_help.py -q@{utt}.
@{bullet}Browser-side harnesses in @{tt}tests/js/@{utt} run from pytest
through @{tt}node@{utt}; without node they are skipped.

@{h2}What a good test does

@{bullet}It goes through the real path: tools through
@{tt}call_with_status@{utt} (otherwise @{tt}_status@{utt} is
@{tt}None@{utt}), configuration through @{tt}load_settings()@{utt} and
@{tt}get_tool_server_config()@{utt}.
@{bullet}Caches and storage go to @{tt}tmp_path@{utt}; otherwise
@{tt}PluginCache@{utt} writes into @{tt}data/cache@{utt}.
@{bullet}@{b}Every new test gets a mutation check@{ub}: break the
production line it guards; the test must fail. A test that stays green
measures nothing.

@{h2}Guards worth knowing

@{table}
Test | Guards
@{tt}tests/plugins/test_status_end_lines.py@{utt} | the status end line of every plugin tool
@{tt}tests/config/test_prompts_have_no_ticking_clock.py@{utt} | no clock in prompt templates
@{tt}tests/agent/test_agent_step_budget_note.py@{utt} | every LLM request is a prefix of the next
@{tt}tests/config/test_no_hardcoded_data_dir.py@{utt} | no spelled-out data paths
@{tt}tests/config/test_config_schemas.py@{utt} | @{tt}schemas/@{utt} matches the config models
@{tt}tests/pluginsystem/test_plugin_deps_aggregation.py@{utt} | plugin dependencies are aggregated
@{tt}tests/pluginsystem/test_plugin_command_declarations.py@{utt} | slash command declarations
@{tt}tests/ui/test_kit.py@{utt} | no native dialogs in panels
@{tt}tests/ui/test_help.py@{utt} | every guide reads cleanly: links, commands, pens
@{body}

@{h2}Conventions

@{bullet}Everything in a code file is English: identifiers, comments,
docstrings, tool descriptions, log messages; commit messages too.
@{bullet}Destructive jobs (cleanup, TTL, sweeps) are opt-in: off by default,
in @{tt}schema.yaml@{utt} and in the code.
@{bullet}Inserted messages carry @{tt}injected_by@{utt}; nothing that ticks
goes into a prompt (@{" Hooks " link hooks}).
@{bullet}Arguments are validated by the tool, errors are returned as
@{tt}{"status": "error", "error": ...}@{utt} (@{" Tools " link tools}).
@{bullet}No provider tables in LLM plugins (@{" LLM " link llm}).
@{bullet}A plugin that a model calls documents its Model Experience in
its guide (@{" Writing a plugin " link plugins}).
@endnode

@node help "Using this help"
@{h1}Using this help

This is an AmigaGuide viewer. AmigaGuide is the hypertext help format of
AmigaOS: a guide is one text file holding a database of pages, called
nodes. Buttons in the text lead to other nodes, in the same guide or in
another one.

@{h2}Where help opens

@{bullet}The question mark at the top right of ScarabHive opens this manual.

@{bullet}A panel whose plugin has a guide or a README shows a question mark
as well: at the end of the dock's tab bar for the tab in front, and in the
bar of a detached window. It opens that plugin's guide here -- or, when
this viewer shows that guide already, brings it forward on the page you
are reading.

@{h2}The buttons above the page

@{table}
Button | Leads to
Contents | the table of contents of the guide you are reading
Index | the guide's index; a guide without one gets an alphabetical list of its nodes
Help | this page, unless a guide names a help node of its own
Retrace | back to the page you came from
Browse < and > | the previous and the next node in reading order
@{body}

@{h2}Buttons in the text

@{bullet}A button opens the node it names, or a documentation file next to
the guide.

@{bullet}A button with an arrow opens a web page in a new browser tab.

@{bullet}A red, crossed-out button leads nowhere: its target does not exist.
That is a mistake in the guide, and the page lists it below itself.

@{bullet}A grey button stands for something that would run a program on an
Amiga (system, rx and the like). A guide here is a document; that button
does nothing.

@{h2}Search

The search field looks through every guide at once: this manual, the
plugin guides and the plugin READMEs. It searches by meaning, not by the
exact words: "how do I start a sub-agent" finds the Sub-Agent Manager
although no page puts it that way. The closest nodes come first. A query
that names a guide (@{tt}sub_agent_manager@{utt}) or a node
(@{tt}scarabhive/help@{utt}) shows that node above the list.

The search understands English best; ask in English.

The very first search builds an index of every guide, which takes a few
seconds. It is kept on disk, and later only a guide that changed is
indexed again. Where the model behind it cannot be loaded (no network on
that first use), the search falls back on the words: a node then matches
when it contains all of them.

In the web chat, @{tt}/help <topic>@{utt} opens this search for the topic.
In the terminal chat it lists the hits to choose from, and a guide or node
named exactly opens straight away.

@{h2}Addresses

The panel's address names the page on screen (@{tt}?guide=...&node=...@{utt}),
so a copied link or a new browser tab opens the same page. Ctrl+click opens
a button in a new tab.

@{h2}What there is to read

@{code}
  @{" ScarabHive manual  " link main}   this guide
  @{" Plugins            " link plugins/main}   every plugin, with its guide or its README
  @{" Writing a guide    " link authoring}   how to document a plugin
  @{" Beyond AmigaOS     " link markdown}   headings, lists, tables, code and more
@{body}
@endnode

@node authoring "Writing a guide"
@{h1}Writing a guide

@{h2}Where a guide lies

@{table}
File | Shows up as
@{tt}docs/guides/<name>.guide@{utt} | part of the manual; its id is @{tt}<name>@{utt}
@{tt}<plugin folder>/<folder name>.guide@{utt} | the plugin's guide; its id is the folder name, which is the plugin type
@{body}

A plugin without a guide is shown with its @{tt}README.md@{utt} until it gets
one, rendered as Markdown -- that is the README's own format. Its links to
Markdown files under @{tt}docs/@{utt} open in the viewer as well; a file
there that no guide, README or linked page links to does not. The
@{" Plugins " link plugins/main} list is generated from
@{tt}plugins.plugin_dirs@{utt} and links each plugin to whichever of the two it
has.

The viewer reads a guide again when its file, or a file it embeds, changes:
save, click, and the change is there.

@{h2}A minimal plugin guide

@{code text}
\@database "my_plugin"
\@author "Your Name"
\@$VER: my_plugin.guide 1.0 (28.09.2026)
\@smartwrap

\@node main "My plugin"
\@{h1}My plugin
What the plugin does, in a paragraph. Lines up to the next blank
line are joined into one paragraph and reflowed to the panel width.

\@{" Configuration " link config}
\@endnode

\@node config "Configuration"
...
\@endnode
@{body}

@{h2}Commands on a line of their own

@{table}
Command | What it does
\@database "name" | the guide's name
\@author, \@(c), \@$VER: | author, copyright, version
\@node name "title" | starts a node; names ignore case
\@endnode | ends it
\@title "title" | the node's title, in place of the one \@node gives
\@toc node | where Contents leads (default: main)
\@prev node, \@next node | where Browse leads (default: the order in the file)
\@index node | where Index leads (default: a node called index, else a generated list)
\@help node | where Help leads (default: this manual's help page)
\@wordwrap | every line is a paragraph that wraps
\@smartwrap | lines up to a blank line are one paragraph
\@embed file | a file inside the node: a Markdown file rendered, any other a code block
\@remark text | a comment, not shown
@{body}

\@index, \@help, \@wordwrap and \@smartwrap before the first node apply to
the whole guide, inside a node to that node. Without \@wordwrap or
\@smartwrap, lines are shown exactly as written and do not wrap.

@{h2}Inside the text

@{table}
Write | For
\@{b} \@{ub}, \@{i} \@{ui}, \@{u} \@{uu} | bold, italic, underline on and off
\@{plain} | all of them off
\@{fg pen}, \@{bg pen} | colour; the pens are text, shine, shadow, fill, filltext, background, highlight
\@{jleft}, \@{jcenter}, \@{jright} | alignment of the lines that follow
\@{line}, \@{par}, \@{tab} | line break, paragraph break, tab
\@{code} ... \@{body} | lines as written, no wrapping: button menus, aligned text
\@{" label " link node} | a button to a node of this guide
\@{" label " link other/node} | to a node of another guide
\@{" label " link node 12} | to line 12 of the guide file, in that node
\@{" label " link docs/design.md} | to a documentation file next to the guide
\\\@ and \\\\ | a literal \@, a literal \\
@{body}

@{tt}other@{utt} is the id of a guide: a manual file's name or a plugin's
folder name. Amiga style paths work as well: @{tt}other.guide/node@{utt},
@{tt}HELP:other.guide/node@{utt}. Headings, lists, tables, code blocks,
images and web links: @{" Beyond AmigaOS " link markdown}.

Buttons that would run something on an Amiga (system, rx, rxs, beep, close,
quit) are shown but do nothing: the viewer never runs what a guide says.

@{h2}A guide inside a plugin's panel

A panel can show its plugin's guide right where it is needed, with the
same buttons, links and Retrace:

@{code html}
<script type="module" src="/static/kit/guide.js"></script>
<pk-guide guide="my_plugin" node="config"></pk-guide>
@{body}

The element comes with the UI kit; the UI Kit panel shows it. This Help
panel is the same element, filling the page.

@{h2}Checking a guide

A mistake does not stop a guide from showing. The panel lists the mistakes
of a node below it: a link that leads nowhere, an image that is not there,
an unknown attribute or pen, something that does not belong in a table
cell or a code block, an \@{ without its closing brace. Mistakes in the structure -- a missing \@endnode, an unknown command,
a node defined twice, an \@embed that finds no file -- go to the server
log. This test checks every guide in the repository for both:

@{code bash}
.venv/Scripts/python.exe -m pytest tests/ui/test_help.py -k repository
@{body}
@endnode

@node markdown "Beyond AmigaOS"
@{h1}Beyond AmigaOS

This viewer reads more than the Amiga did: what Markdown has, written in
AmigaGuide's own syntax. A guide stays an AmigaGuide file; only a Markdown
file -- a README, a linked @{tt}docs/*.md@{utt} -- is shown as Markdown.

@{h2}Paragraphs of a kind

A paragraph starts with one of these at the start of a line. It needs no
blank line before it. With \@smartwrap a list item or a quote runs until a
blank line or the next one starts; without it every line is a paragraph of
its own. Numbers count on across blank lines, code blocks and tables; a
plain paragraph, a heading, a quote, a rule or a bullet on the same level
starts them anew.

@{table}
Write | Shows
\@{h1}Title, \@{h2}, \@{h3} | a heading; it ends with its line
\@{bullet}item | a bullet list item; \@{bullet 2} one level in
\@{number}item | a numbered item, counted by itself; \@{number 2} one level in
\@{quote}text | a quoted paragraph
\@{rule} | a line across the page
@{body}

@{code text}
\@{h2}Setup
\@{number}Create the virtual environment.
\@{number}Install it: \@{tt}pip install -e .\@{utt}
\@{bullet 2}add \@{tt}[dev]\@{utt} for the developer tools
\@{number}Start the API.
@{body}

shows as

@{h2}Setup
@{number}Create the virtual environment.
@{number}Install it: @{tt}pip install -e .@{utt}
@{bullet 2}add @{tt}[dev]@{utt} for the developer tools
@{number}Start the API.

@{h2}In the text

@{table}
Write | Shows
\@{tt}code\@{utt} | inline code: a path, a command, a key
\@{s}text\@{us} | struck-through text
\@{image file.png "alt text"} | an image next to the guide, shown in place
\@{" label " link https://...} | a web link, opened in a new tab (http, https, mailto)
@{body}

@{h2}Code blocks

@{code text}
\@{code yaml}
plugins:
  servers:
    my_plugin: {type: my_plugin, enabled: true}
\@{body}
@{body}

shows as

@{code yaml}
plugins:
  servers:
    my_plugin: {type: my_plugin, enabled: true}
@{body}

A language after @{tt}\@{code@{utt} makes a code block, coloured by its
language (python, yaml, json, toml, bash, sql, javascript, typescript, css,
html, xml, diff, markdown, c, cpp, java). A block with a button, an image
or an attribute in it -- also one still switched on from before, such as an
unclosed @{tt}\@{b}@{utt} -- keeps them and stays uncoloured. A page colours
at most 100,000 characters of code; a block that no longer fits stays
plain. @{tt}\@{code}@{utt} alone
keeps its AmigaOS meaning -- lines as written, no wrapping -- for button
menus and aligned text.

@{h2}Tables

@{code text}
\@{table}
Command | What it does
\@{tt}agent-cli run "task"\@{utt} | one task, then exit
\@{tt}agent-cli chat\@{utt} | an interactive session
\@{body}
@{body}

One row per line, cells split by @{tt}|@{utt}, the first row is the header;
a literal @{tt}|@{utt} in a cell is @{tt}\\|@{utt}. Buttons and attributes
work inside a cell; a line break, a block or an alignment does not. A
Markdown separator line right under the header (@{tt}|---|---|@{utt}) is
allowed once and ignored.

@{h2}Images and embedded files

An image lies at the top of the guide's folder or under its
@{tt}docs/@{utt} folder: @{tt}diagram.png@{utt} and
@{tt}docs/diagram.png@{utt} work, @{tt}images/diagram.png@{utt} and
@{tt}../other/diagram.png@{utt} do not. Images may be PNG, JPEG, GIF, WebP
or SVG; a remote image is not loaded. The alt text is what a screen reader
says and what the page shows when the file is missing. An embedded file
lies in the guide's folder or below it, never above. A file larger than
512 KB is named, not shown.

@{tt}\@embed@{utt} is AmigaOS's own command: the file becomes part of the
node and is never read as guide commands. A Markdown file is rendered, any
other file is a code block in its language -- so a guide can show a real
configuration file instead of a copy that goes stale.

Back to @{" Writing a guide " link authoring}.
@endnode
